00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:05.770
Welcome to "Ear Biscuits", I'm Link.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:06.603
And I'm Rhett.

00:00:06.603 --> 00:00:08.560
This week at the Round
Table of dim lighting,

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.419
we are talking all about what
we call our other best friend,

00:00:13.419 --> 00:00:18.330
the trio that was

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:19.780
Rhett, Link and Ben.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.409
Ben Greenwood, our childhood friend,

00:00:22.409 --> 00:00:24.950
who we've talked about on and off,

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:25.783
from time to time.

00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:27.000
Yeah, he's come up a lot.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.604
But we're gonna devote an entire episode

00:00:28.604 --> 00:00:29.708
to talking about Ben.

00:00:29.708 --> 00:00:33.362
Yeah, this is an important episode for us.

00:00:33.362 --> 00:00:36.472
I mean, maybe,

00:00:36.472 --> 00:00:39.040
I hope it will be for you too listening

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.091
because, I mean, whether
it's a childhood friend

00:00:42.091 --> 00:00:46.620
or someone that you meet
along the way in your life,

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.500
I think that there, there are
people that have an impact

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.670
on your life, and sometimes
it's not until you look back

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:57.380
on all those shared
experiences can you really

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:00.310
piece together how deep that impact goes.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:04.883
So, I'm looking forward to this episode.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.750
Over the, I was gonna say
years, I guess over the years,

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.180
there's been times when
I've thought I wanna,

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.712
I wanna do what we're doing today.

00:01:13.712 --> 00:01:18.712
I wanna, I wanna talk about
our friendship with Ben,

00:01:18.953 --> 00:01:23.953
and just pull it all
together just for the sake of

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.647
taking away even more from it
and appreciate it even more.

00:01:30.647 --> 00:01:32.467
I hope that's what happens today and.

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:37.467
Yeah, because we've, we've
talked about and we have,

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.521
as we're about to
demonstrate, incorporated Ben

00:01:39.521 --> 00:01:42.387
into our work.

00:01:42.387 --> 00:01:47.387
We've also talked about, he
just, he was such an influence

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:53.990
on us and the way that we
lived our lives as kids,

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.320
which has translated into the
way that we live our lives

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.790
as adults, which I think
is all about mythicality.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:00.623
Such, that--

00:02:00.623 --> 00:02:02.698
Absolutely, like, yeah, when we wrote

00:02:02.698 --> 00:02:05.770
our "Book of Mythicality",
which if you don't know,

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.243
we wrote a book, it's part
coffee table, part autobiography,

00:02:10.243 --> 00:02:12.401
lots of stories, lots of illustrations.

00:02:12.401 --> 00:02:13.850
Get it for somebody.

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:15.610
Get it for yourself.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.340
I didn't mean, get it for
yourself if you don't have it.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.020
When you say get it for
somebody, makes it sound like,

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.040
