00:06:53.644 --> 00:06:57.347
And this is something
that now I've kind of seen

00:06:57.347 --> 00:07:00.270
multiple kids do in
different movies, and stuff,

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:02.016
but the way he talked
about his grandfather

00:07:02.016 --> 00:07:05.150
was as if his grandfather was the biggest,

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:07.089
baddest badass in town,

00:07:07.089 --> 00:07:09.824
who could beat up anybody
including his own dad.

00:07:09.824 --> 00:07:11.790
And just he was like,

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.965
he would just talk about his grandfather,

00:07:13.965 --> 00:07:16.217
like his grandfather was
this larger than life

00:07:16.217 --> 00:07:19.560
guy who knew everything
and was incredibly strong.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.000
Like the strength of Ben's
grandfather was something

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.670
that was legendary that
was like, and you know,

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:27.290
this is before the age of information,

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:29.057
like the internet was just kind of get--

00:07:29.057 --> 00:07:30.269
You couldn't Google his grandfather

00:07:30.269 --> 00:07:31.840
to validate these claims.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:33.550
You couldn't Google Oklahoma and be like,

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.902
oh, Oklahoma is just a state above Texas

00:07:35.902 --> 00:07:37.350
that's shaped like a pot.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:38.187
Right.

00:07:38.187 --> 00:07:41.140
Nothing against Oklahoma,
but it's just another state.

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:44.532
It's as magical as any other
state, but it's pretty flat.

00:07:44.532 --> 00:07:47.140
He had a way of making--

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:48.430
Yes, his imagination,

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:50.373
was incredible.
Everything magical.

00:07:52.661 --> 00:07:53.680
I was immediately
captivated, and I was like,

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:55.330
I wanna be around this kid.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:58.760
His dad, his family was
interesting because his dad

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:00.940
was a professor at Campbell,
just like your dad, right?

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:03.500
He was a biology professor, am I right?

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:04.863
I think he was in the,

00:08:06.312 --> 00:08:07.580
he was in the pharmacy school maybe?

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:08.734
Or maybe biology.

00:08:08.734 --> 00:08:10.230
I think they all had the same building,

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:13.360
he was in a scientific
discipline of some sort, yes.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:14.193
Okay.

00:08:16.103 --> 00:08:21.103
But the, behind his house
was a whole stretch of woods.

00:08:23.655 --> 00:08:25.085
And Ben would always
go out into the woods.

00:08:25.085 --> 00:08:28.692
And I remember when the three
of us started hanging out

00:08:28.692 --> 00:08:31.660
about every, I don't know,
I got the impression that

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:35.490
about every three to five times
that you guys would hang out

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:38.120
I would also be there because
I had farther to come,

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:38.953
and you know.

00:08:38.953 --> 00:08:40.320
Right, and you were,

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:43.123
you were a little scared
of things at that time.

00:08:43.123 --> 00:08:44.283
Just at that time.

00:08:45.425 --> 00:08:47.967
You've always been a little
bit scared of things.

00:08:47.967 --> 00:08:49.500
Ben was not scared of anything.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.333
He wasn't scared of anything.

00:08:50.333 --> 00:08:53.650
I mean, he was always
gallivanting through the woods

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:56.372
and there was a if you go
deep enough into the woods,

00:08:56.372 --> 00:08:59.300
there was a creek that, and actually,

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:00.841
it was Buies Creek.

00:09:00.841 --> 00:09:01.690
It was Buies Creek, yeah.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:03.400
That ran back there.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:08.210
And just like all the other
times, there was something crazy

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:11.258
that we embarked upon, it
was most likely Ben's idea

00:09:11.258 --> 00:09:16.258
at this point to build
a dam to dam the creek

00:09:17.064 --> 00:09:19.090
in order to what?

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:21.323
Make a little pond that then.

00:09:21.323 --> 00:09:22.156
Put fish in it.

00:09:22.156 --> 00:09:23.965
Put fish, stock it with fish.

00:09:23.965 --> 00:09:26.385
To stock a pond, and also to build a raft,

00:09:26.385 --> 00:09:29.730
to raft out throughout our pond.

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:32.478
So we did all three of these things.

00:09:32.478 --> 00:09:35.492
In different places at the same time.

00:09:35.492 --> 00:09:39.524
But so we went back there and
we dedicated an entire day.

00:09:39.524 --> 00:09:40.684
Like beavers, man.

00:09:40.684 --> 00:09:43.930
Just taking rocks, taking logs,

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:45.850
taking everything we could scrounge up.

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:47.229
Well, we found a plot,
In the woods.

00:09:47.229 --> 00:09:49.630
To give you an idea,
_ To throw into the creek.

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:53.530
Like, Buies Creek, when it
was just typically flowing,

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:55.758
this is one of those
creeks that probably like

00:09:55.758 --> 00:10:00.120
10 feet across, maybe
20 feet in some places,

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:01.415
maybe five feet in other places.

00:10:01.415 --> 00:10:05.240
But in the middle of the
summer, it's not flowing,

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:06.829
