00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:43.200
We fish down there all the time.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.350
You can catch some bass,
some brim and some crappie.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:47.130
We got some crappy, put them in a bucket,

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:47.963
brought them up, put them in there,

00:11:47.963 --> 00:11:49.150
they swim around for a little bit,

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:50.080
and then the dam broke again,

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:51.160
because that's what happens to dams.

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:52.320
I wasn't much of a fisherman.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.180
So I kind of, I think--

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:58.360
A lot of my time spent with
Ben was fishing because--

00:11:58.360 --> 00:11:59.500
He was an outdoorsman.

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:00.333
He was.

00:12:00.333 --> 00:12:03.592
Because we also, we cut, you
know, we've talked about this,

00:12:03.592 --> 00:12:06.017
about how we would just
find a tree and cut it down.

00:12:06.017 --> 00:12:09.201
Sometimes just going and finding a tree,

00:12:09.201 --> 00:12:12.551
just selecting a tree in the woods

00:12:12.551 --> 00:12:15.040
and spending all day with an ax

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:17.200
trading off cutting down this giant tree,

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:18.480
which is incredibly dangerous,

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:21.420
not to mention, not really
environmentally responsible

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:23.940
because we were just timbering
one tree for nothing.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.274
But he owned an ax.

00:12:26.274 --> 00:12:27.107
Yeah.

00:12:27.107 --> 00:12:28.958
And he sharpened his ax.

00:12:28.958 --> 00:12:32.410
And he invited us to
trudge into the woods,

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:33.390
pick the perfect tree,

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:34.830
and it was a big one.

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.471
Again, this is an all day endeavor,

00:12:36.471 --> 00:12:39.477
taking, trading off,
chopping this thing down.

00:12:39.477 --> 00:12:42.339
It was absolutely exhausting.

00:12:42.339 --> 00:12:43.575
Right.

00:12:43.575 --> 00:12:45.100
And when that thing started to creek.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:46.198
Hours of not talking.

00:12:46.198 --> 00:12:47.100
Yeah.

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:48.380
Of just chopping.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:50.060
But we learned, like we learned like,

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:52.890
oh, if you want to cut down
a tree like you just don't,

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:53.890
you got to like create,

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:55.870
you got to like come in
from the top at an angle,

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:57.700
you had to go in from
the bottom at an angle.

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:00.620
Like we kind of learned by just doing

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:01.690
how you chop down a tree.

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:03.957
I mean, I haven't had to use
that skill yet again, but--

00:13:03.957 --> 00:13:06.810
And you're talking and we're talking like,

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:08.812
seventh grade, maybe sixth grade.

00:13:08.812 --> 00:13:09.650
Right.

00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:11.273
But we're the raft thing,

00:13:11.273 --> 00:13:15.140
Ben cut down a tree about
that big, a pine tree.

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:16.049
Okay.

00:13:16.049 --> 00:13:17.140
And then he cut it up.

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.410
And then we took ropes
and we tied it together.

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:21.260
And then we tried to float it on the pond.

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:22.093
I wasn't there for that.

00:13:22.093 --> 00:13:23.310
And the whole thing came apart

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:24.690
as soon as we tried to float it,

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:26.160
like when we tried to get on it,

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:27.167
it was like, this isn't stable enough.

00:13:27.167 --> 00:13:29.730
I can guarantee you if I was there,

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:30.806
it still wouldn't have worked.

00:13:30.806 --> 00:13:32.410
You know what I'm saying?

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:33.740
But he just the idea.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:35.617
He would build a lean to, right?

00:13:35.617 --> 00:13:37.860
Well, yeah, so that's, uh,

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.570
that's one of my favorite
stories about Ben

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:45.570
is the time that he, there
was a an empty lot across,

00:13:45.705 --> 00:13:48.430
well, basically woods across
the street from his house.

00:13:48.430 --> 00:13:51.010
Now, I've gone back, you
know, in the past couple years

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:53.130
and now that whole side of
the street and Keith Hills

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:53.963
is filled with houses,

00:13:53.963 --> 00:13:55.880
but for a long time it was just woods.

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:57.030
Yeah.

00:13:57.030 --> 00:13:59.360
And he said,

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:01.370
I go to his house, he's like,

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:02.920
I think we should build an A frame.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:06.170
And I'm like, yeah, I have no idea

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:08.110
what the hell an A frame is.

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:09.320
But I'm like, yeah, that's right, A frame.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:10.260
Let's do it.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:11.093
Okay.

00:14:11.093 --> 00:14:14.089
And so again,

00:14:14.089 --> 00:14:16.140
the thing that was so
mysterious about Ben was

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:18.656
in a day before the internet,

00:14:18.656 --> 00:14:21.360
how did people learn things?

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:22.715
He just learned things in books.

00:14:22.715 --> 00:14:24.958
He just, I guess he just
went to the library,

00:14:24.958 --> 00:14:27.840
which nobody else was doing.

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:30.500
And we're just be like, I got this.

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:31.980
I know how to build A frame.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:33.374
I don't even know what part.

00:14:33.374 --> 00:14:37.940
It wasn't until we finished
and it wasn't A frame

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:39.170
by looking at it from the side,

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:40.330
I was like, oh, A frame.

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:41.987
The whole time, I was like, yeah, A frame,

00:14:41.987 --> 00:14:43.380
and I was like, where's the A?

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:46.110
But we spent all day building this thing

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.020
out of two by fours.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:50.293
And oh, not limbs?

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:55.950
No, we use two by fours as
the braces of this thing.

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:56.783
Okay.

00:14:56.783 --> 00:14:58.147
And like nailed them together.

00:14:58.147 --> 00:15:01.010
But then We got sticks.

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:02.210
Again the whole time,

00:15:02.210 --> 00:15:04.880
I'm kind of acting like
I know what we're doing.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:06.720
You know, I'm saying like, I'm there.

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:07.570
I'm helping,

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:09.490
but I'm kind of like waiting
to see him do something

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:10.590
then to know what we're doing.

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:11.740
And he probably knew all of this.

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:14.630
And then we put sticks, big sticks on it,

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:15.910
leaned up against each other,

00:15:15.910 --> 00:15:16.970
then smaller sticks.

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:19.843
And then we made a giant pit of mud.

00:15:22.686 --> 00:15:25.760
And then started putting the
mud on top of all the sticks,

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:27.087
and then put leaves on top of that,

00:15:27.087 --> 00:15:28.910
and like straw and grass and stuff.

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:29.760
Yeah.

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:32.956
Ben was like, that guy from primitive.

00:15:32.956 --> 00:15:34.569
What's it called that YouTube channel?

00:15:34.569 --> 00:15:35.670
Primitive technology.

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:36.670
Primitive technology.

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:38.123
Yeah, he would be that guy.

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:42.578
If he had a YouTube channel, and so he,

00:15:42.578 --> 00:15:45.530
we did this and then of course,

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:47.470
he was like, we're gonna do this.

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:49.897
And then we're gonna sleep in there.

00:15:49.897 --> 00:15:51.280
Camp?

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:54.269
And so that night, after
we finished the thing,

00:15:54.269 --> 00:15:57.110
we like kind of like
cleaned out underneath it.

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:58.411
We put our sleeping bags under there.

00:15:58.411 --> 00:16:01.310
And we camped this A frame,

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:02.800
and I just thought,

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:05.270
this is the coolest thing I've ever done.

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:08.860
And every everything
that Ben set out to do

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:10.050
was the coolest thing we'd ever done.

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:11.746
Yeah, well and that A frame,

00:16:11.746 --> 00:16:15.240
for many, many, many years,

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:16.918
we probably did that in like fifth grade.

00:16:16.918 --> 00:16:21.103
Even when I would come
back home from college,

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:24.060
I would go out there
and it'd be like, man,

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:24.893
there's still like,

00:16:24.893 --> 00:16:26.490
I mean, I wouldn't sleep
under there anymore.

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:29.343
But like there's like mud.

00:16:29.343 --> 00:16:31.507
The roof is kind of still intact.

00:16:31.507 --> 00:16:34.093
It was like it was it wasn't A frame man.

00:16:34.093 --> 00:16:35.090
It held up.

00:16:35.090 --> 00:16:37.540
So I mean, if you've read the
"Lost Causes of Bleak Creek"

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:40.716
now you see the connection
between a kid who can escape

00:16:40.716 --> 00:16:42.870
from a reformed school,

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:44.860
and instead of going
back home to his parents,

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:49.360
just subsists on his own in the woods.

00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:50.212
Yeah, eating squirrels.

00:16:50.212 --> 00:16:51.796
Not something that we made up.

00:16:51.796 --> 00:16:53.970
Yeah, he could easily have done all that.

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:58.970
I remember you two taking
me out to this A frame,

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:01.000
which in my mind was a lean to,

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:01.977
but I guess it leaned on the other to.

00:17:01.977 --> 00:17:03.620
It leaned on to itself.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:04.730
Yeah.
Made an A.

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:07.500
And then you said, would you want,

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:09.403
would you like to smoke a big George?

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:14.700
And I'm like, ah, I'm afraid.

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:15.660
Yeah.

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:20.660
So again, this idea came from
the fact that Ben was so,

00:17:23.450 --> 00:17:25.290
again, he's reading some book

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:27.770
and he's like, hey, you want
to make some Juniper tea?

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:29.245
I'm like, yes.

00:17:29.245 --> 00:17:31.770
And so he's like, oh, we got
to go get these Juniper bushes

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:34.810
and we got to put them in water
and steep it and make tea,

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:36.330
and it tasted like absolute crap.

00:17:36.330 --> 00:17:37.163
It was horrible,

00:17:37.163 --> 00:17:38.350
made you want to vomit.

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:41.080
But he would have these
things that he would make

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:43.137
that were sort of the "My
Side of the Mountain".

00:17:43.137 --> 00:17:43.970
"My Side of the Mountain"

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:44.880
was my favorite book growing up,

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:46.110
and I kind of saw Ben as that kid.

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:47.534
I wanted to be that kid,
but I saw Ben as the kid.

00:17:47.534 --> 00:17:49.180
Yeah.

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:50.013
Who could do these things.

00:17:50.013 --> 00:17:52.620
And that kid lived in a hollowed out tree.

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:53.580
Hollow tree.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:56.660
And so we had we had done all this stuff,

00:17:56.660 --> 00:17:58.157
and it hadn't necessarily
tasted good or whatever.

00:17:58.157 --> 00:18:01.750
But then in that same
woodsy area over there

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:02.583
we built A frame,

00:18:02.583 --> 00:18:04.897
there where the stalks of--

00:18:04.897 --> 00:18:06.100
It was like a weed.

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:08.358
It was like a weed that
basically was like bamboo.

00:18:08.358 --> 00:18:12.826
Yeah, very rigid.
It was a rigid,

00:18:12.826 --> 00:18:16.351
sort of like sectioned off bamboo like--

00:18:16.351 --> 00:18:17.184
Hull.

00:18:17.184 --> 00:18:18.090
Shaft.

00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:19.593
And it would, it dried up,

00:18:19.593 --> 00:18:21.446
it was very hard.

00:18:21.446 --> 00:18:24.483
Yeah, it would it would
grow and then it would die.

00:18:24.483 --> 00:18:25.460
And you could snap it off.

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:27.080
Snap it off,

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:29.796
and then I, look, I
mean I'm gonna give Ben

00:18:29.796 --> 00:18:32.083
the credit on this one, he can take it,

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:35.213
one day he decided

00:18:35.213 --> 00:18:37.713
what if we smoked this,

00:18:37.713 --> 00:18:40.550
like a, but it wasn't
like a let's smoke this

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:42.010
like was smoke weed.

00:18:42.010 --> 00:18:44.930
It was more like let's smoke
this like a peace pipe.

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:45.763
Yeah.

00:18:45.763 --> 00:18:46.683
Ceremonial
Like it was,

00:18:46.683 --> 00:18:47.858
more like a native american type thing,

00:18:47.858 --> 00:18:51.830
finding something in the
earth and smoking it.

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:56.092
But it, I mean it was a hollow device

00:18:56.092 --> 00:18:57.700
that could be used for smoking

00:18:57.700 --> 00:18:59.549
if you put something in
it that was worth smoking.

00:18:59.549 --> 00:19:00.382
But we didn't.

00:19:00.382 --> 00:19:01.840
When you offered it to me,

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:03.840
it was empty and you
just lit the end of it.

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:05.040
You lit the end--
With a match.

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:06.800
So you suck the smoke through

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:09.493
and it was acrid.

00:19:09.493 --> 00:19:12.750
It was smoking a big George, man.

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:14.698
We didn't do anything for you

00:19:14.698 --> 00:19:15.685
except make you cough a lot.

00:19:15.685 --> 00:19:16.780
Well yeah.

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:18.497
And when you ask me to do it,

00:19:18.497 --> 00:19:22.560
I do remember that neither
one of you were also doing it.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:23.393
It wasn't--

00:19:23.393 --> 00:19:24.522
We've done this at at length.

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:26.663
Right.

00:19:26.663 --> 00:19:27.860
We've smoked big George.

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:29.827
But I mean, I just felt

00:19:29.827 --> 00:19:33.210
this great sense of privilege

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:35.660
to be hanging out with you guys

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:37.230
when it was like oh man,

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:38.730
you've built this thing,

00:19:38.730 --> 00:19:41.210
and I'm getting invited
to be a part of this.

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:43.350
It's like, I mean on one hand,

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:46.450
I was like man, I'm
missing out on some fun.

00:19:46.450 --> 00:19:48.070
But I'm glad to be here

00:19:48.070 --> 00:19:49.180
for whatever's going on.

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:51.821
On the days that he wasn't
carrying around an ax,

00:19:51.821 --> 00:19:55.010
he would always carry around a machete.

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:55.843
Machete.

00:19:55.843 --> 00:19:58.429
And then we each went to Wamble and Sons

00:19:58.429 --> 00:19:59.906
and bought our own machetes

00:19:59.906 --> 00:20:01.140
'cause you're going through,

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:03.417
there's no paths unless you make them.

00:20:03.417 --> 00:20:05.687
And we would always go explore the woods.

00:20:05.687 --> 00:20:09.294
And there would be these huge vine

00:20:09.294 --> 00:20:12.028
that would grow up into the trees.

00:20:12.028 --> 00:20:14.220
And the vines would, I mean,

00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:16.460
it would be as big around
as a silver dollar.

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:17.510
Some of them would be huge.

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:18.512
That's an interesting measurement.

00:20:18.512 --> 00:20:20.854
Big around as a silver dollar.

00:20:20.854 --> 00:20:24.670
Those were the, a silver dollar diameter

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:27.766
is the perfect diameter for a vine.

00:20:27.766 --> 00:20:28.599
I think a little bit bigger than that.

00:20:28.599 --> 00:20:29.432
In fact, I'll tell,

00:20:29.432 --> 00:20:31.900
I have the story of something
that happened to me on a vine

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:34.065
that we will tell you in just one second.

00:20:34.065 --> 00:20:36.350
But first, we're gonna let you know

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:38.333
that you can get this new shirt

00:20:38.333 --> 00:20:40.620
that Link is wearing--
Snuck it out on me.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:41.716
Yeah I did.

00:20:41.716 --> 00:20:42.560
I was in the middle of a story.

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:44.413
Yeah, well, see, I added a teaser.

00:20:44.413 --> 00:20:46.610
And yeah.
Read that.

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:50.218
This is I was a teenage mythical beast.

00:20:50.218 --> 00:20:53.173
This is our homage to,

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:55.043
can we, I mean,

00:20:55.043 --> 00:20:57.310
it's really an homage to
Teen Wolf, but it's not.

00:20:57.310 --> 00:20:58.143
I don't think we can say that.

00:20:58.143 --> 00:20:59.760
It's not Teen Wolf, you know,

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:01.109
'cause of like copyrights.
It's cool though.

00:21:01.109 --> 00:21:03.720
It's a and original creation.

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.183
Mythical.com.

00:21:05.183 --> 00:21:08.383
Teenage Mythical beast.

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:12.340
So we would go into the
woods with our machetes,

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:13.980
and you're not just chopping
down to make a path,

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.052
but you see these vines growing up,

00:21:16.052 --> 00:21:18.388
and you chop the bottom,

00:21:18.388 --> 00:21:21.210
and then you walk, you grab it

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:24.410
and you walk aways until it
starts getting so taught,

00:21:24.410 --> 00:21:26.810
you know it's like cause it's anchored up

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:28.055
into the top of the tree,

00:21:28.055 --> 00:21:30.830
and then you just, you jump

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:32.630
and you swing like Tarzan.

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:34.530
And around the Cape Fear river,

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:37.216
and around Buies Creek,
all behind Ben's house,

00:21:37.216 --> 00:21:40.584
the place was full of these vines.

00:21:40.584 --> 00:21:43.916
And if you found the exact right diameter,

00:21:43.916 --> 00:21:47.000
and you found it anchored
to the right tree

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:49.609
with the right topography,

00:21:49.609 --> 00:21:54.609
you could swing just an epic distance.

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:56.850
We found one when we
were back in Buies Creek,

00:21:56.850 --> 00:21:58.680
but I don't think it made the edit.

00:21:58.680 --> 00:21:59.540
We didn't have a machete.

00:21:59.540 --> 00:22:01.230
We didn't have anything
to cut it at the bottom.

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:02.672
And then the one that we found was,

00:22:02.672 --> 00:22:06.203
it was broken, and when, that kills it.

00:22:06.203 --> 00:22:08.198
Well yeah, once you cut the vine,

00:22:08.198 --> 00:22:10.500
we actually had these theories

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:11.610
about can you cut the vine

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:12.940
and can we bring a bucket of water

00:22:12.940 --> 00:22:14.430
and set the vine in the bucket of water

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:15.560
to make the vine live longer

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:16.800
so we can swing on it more.

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:17.667
Yeah.

00:22:17.667 --> 00:22:19.269
But I was always super, you know,

00:22:19.269 --> 00:22:23.675
worried because I was always
the biggest and heaviest one,

00:22:23.675 --> 00:22:25.580
so you know that if
the vine's gonna break,

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:26.680
it's gonna break on the big boy.

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:30.310
Your dad had instilled this awareness,

00:22:30.310 --> 00:22:32.080
or maybe even a fear of injury

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:36.730
because you know, you were a
burgeoning basketball star,

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:39.206
at least in your mind.

00:22:39.206 --> 00:22:41.526
Well in the minds of all those
in Harnett County as well.

00:22:41.526 --> 00:22:42.920
Take the bait.

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:43.753
Take the bait.

00:22:43.753 --> 00:22:45.700
So I remember it 'cause I was,

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:47.420
I was already in high school this time.

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:48.253
Yeah.

00:22:48.253 --> 00:22:50.170
And it was when they were beginning

00:22:50.170 --> 00:22:53.030
to sort of clear out the area

00:22:53.030 --> 00:22:55.720
that then eventually
became the second 18 holes

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:58.080
for Keith Hills, but we didn't
know what they were doing,

00:22:58.080 --> 00:22:59.740
but there was kind of a weird dirt road

00:22:59.740 --> 00:23:01.400
that they had kind of
cut through the woods.

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:04.310
Yeah, this was one of our
last vine swinging adventures.

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:06.410
This is the last time I swung on a vine.

00:23:08.648 --> 00:23:10.410
And like you said, you
find the right topography.

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:11.875
So it was perfect.

00:23:11.875 --> 00:23:13.631
There was a hill,

00:23:13.631 --> 00:23:17.007
and the tree that had a vine on it

00:23:17.007 --> 00:23:19.240
was right next to this road

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:20.700
that was going downhill.

00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:21.834
Dirt road.
Dirt road.

00:23:21.834 --> 00:23:24.840
And we cut the vine,

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:26.030
and you guys started swinging,

00:23:26.030 --> 00:23:27.380
and it was like, it was magical

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:28.740
because it was the kind
of thing you could go

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:30.540
really high up on this hill,

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:33.270
and then swing and then at your pinnacle,

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:35.046
at your peak, you guys were probably

00:23:35.046 --> 00:23:37.570
eight feet off the ground,

00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:38.800
10 feet off the ground.

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:39.861
If not 50.

00:23:39.861 --> 00:23:43.100
And I was nervous,

00:23:43.100 --> 00:23:46.930
it was probably like
sophomore or junior year,

00:23:46.930 --> 00:23:48.594
like well into thinking I'm
gonna play college basketball.

00:23:48.594 --> 00:23:51.370
And I was always worried at that point

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:52.470
about getting injured.

00:23:53.730 --> 00:23:57.093
And I was up, but I can't
say no to this perfect vine.

00:23:57.093 --> 00:24:01.403
So I swung, and I was in
the middle of just like,

00:24:01.403 --> 00:24:04.796
this euphoric meeting,

00:24:04.796 --> 00:24:06.640
the end of the pendulum,

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:09.210
and like, I was up on the vine.

00:24:09.210 --> 00:24:10.606
My knees were in the air.

00:24:10.606 --> 00:24:12.276
That was also a mistake

00:24:12.276 --> 00:24:14.769
because at it's pinnacle,

00:24:14.769 --> 00:24:17.880
the vine just completely gave away.

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:19.109
I mean I kept it in my hands.

00:24:19.109 --> 00:24:20.603
Right, right.

00:24:20.603 --> 00:24:21.550
You hold the vine the whole time.

00:24:21.550 --> 00:24:23.130
But it's like no longer holding on

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:24.530
to whatever branch it needed to.

00:24:24.530 --> 00:24:26.578
Wasn't your fault.

00:24:26.578 --> 00:24:28.220
And I come down on that dirt road,

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:29.310
on my tailbone.

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:31.670
Oh, gosh, I remember seeing it happen.

00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:33.500
And I remember the noise you made.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:34.790
Do you remember all the words, because--

00:24:34.790 --> 00:24:36.060
Oh, oh.

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:37.620
It was like--
You were all the,

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:42.620
I said, I unleashed a tirade of profanity.

00:24:43.004 --> 00:24:44.743
It was, we cursed at times.

00:24:44.743 --> 00:24:49.200
But like I cursed so much
in span of about 30 seconds,

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:50.839
and I was like, I think
I've broken my back.

00:24:50.839 --> 00:24:52.201
I think I've broken my tailbone.

00:24:52.201 --> 00:24:56.500
And it's my theory that my back problems,

00:24:56.500 --> 00:24:57.409
that's when they started.

00:24:57.409 --> 00:24:58.835
But you know, it was all worth it.

00:24:58.835 --> 00:25:03.490
I think I probably compressed
some discs at that point.

00:25:03.490 --> 00:25:04.477
Ben had a canoe.

00:25:04.477 --> 00:25:07.703
We would take the canoe
on the Cape Fear river.

00:25:07.703 --> 00:25:10.640
And his parents, God bless 'em,

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:13.370
would, we'd put the
canoe on top of the car,

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:16.070
and we'd drive Lillington,

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:17.830
or sometimes all the way to Raven Rock,

00:25:17.830 --> 00:25:20.276
and they just wait to say bon voyage.

00:25:20.276 --> 00:25:22.806
And then they'd say we'll pick you up

00:25:22.806 --> 00:25:24.260
in either Buies Creek

00:25:24.260 --> 00:25:25.666
or way down in Irwin,

00:25:25.666 --> 00:25:28.392
like six hours later.

00:25:28.392 --> 00:25:30.290
Well that, well-

00:25:30.290 --> 00:25:31.651
If we did all of that.

00:25:31.651 --> 00:25:33.954
That was the day we went
to Buck Horn Dam though.

00:25:33.954 --> 00:25:36.732
That was when it took hours and hours.

00:25:36.732 --> 00:25:39.273
But we would do all stretches of the river

00:25:39.273 --> 00:25:43.030
in this canoe, and three boys in a canoe,

00:25:43.030 --> 00:25:46.090
it's like, it was amazing.

00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:47.741
Later, we ended up buying kayaks.

00:25:47.741 --> 00:25:50.020
Ben didn't get one for
reasons we'll get into,

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:53.020
but like the love for the river,

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:54.850
and you know, we've
talked about it so much

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:56.560
and how swimming in the river,

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:57.470
crossing the river.

00:25:57.470 --> 00:25:58.917
He did get a kayak.

00:25:58.917 --> 00:25:59.890
Did he get a kayak?

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:00.723
Yeah, 'cause he could actually,

00:26:00.723 --> 00:26:01.930
he was the only one
who could roll it over.

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:03.500
That is right.

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:05.663
Yeah, I don't, I don't
know why I forgot that.

00:26:07.430 --> 00:26:08.360
Our love for the river

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:11.780
was because Ben was the one
who always wanted to be there

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:13.540
and said, yeah, we can
take a canoe out on there.

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:15.777
This is something that we can do.

00:26:15.777 --> 00:26:19.100
He never thought of an idea,

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:20.750
and then said, no, we can't do that.

00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:23.530
He would think of an idea
and we would just do it.

00:26:23.530 --> 00:26:25.990
And, you know, the
interesting thing is that

00:26:25.990 --> 00:26:27.403
I think that there was a,

00:26:29.932 --> 00:26:31.158
because I will say, you know,

00:26:31.158 --> 00:26:34.697
I give Ben a credit for being the one

00:26:34.697 --> 00:26:38.670
that kind of like sparked
this sort of sense

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:43.163
of adventure and sense of imagination.

00:26:43.163 --> 00:26:47.320
But I also, I want to
recognize just the fact

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:49.730
that I think he could have done that,

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:51.350
he could have done that with other kids

00:26:51.350 --> 00:26:53.270
who would have not responded to it,

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:54.510
who would write like,

00:26:54.510 --> 00:26:56.340
but we were, we were on board.

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:57.173
Oh, yeah.

00:26:57.173 --> 00:26:58.077
It was very much,

00:26:58.077 --> 00:27:00.000
and that we kind of kept that same spirit.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:00.833
Like, it's like--
It resonated.

00:27:00.833 --> 00:27:01.860
I don't know how to do this.

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:04.300
He didn't have to talk us in to anything.

00:27:04.300 --> 00:27:05.795
It was very much like yes,

00:27:05.795 --> 00:27:07.434
we're here for this.

00:27:07.434 --> 00:27:09.550
Yes, let's go to Buck Horn Dam.

00:27:09.550 --> 00:27:13.220
There was, we never
stopped to ask the question

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:15.335
is this a good idea.

00:27:15.335 --> 00:27:17.230
Is this safe?

00:27:17.230 --> 00:27:18.831
Might we die?

00:27:18.831 --> 00:27:20.620
And I think--
Right.

00:27:20.620 --> 00:27:22.890
If we had a stopped and
asked those questions,

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:23.940
we probably would've,

00:27:24.908 --> 00:27:26.220
a number of things we wouldn't've done.

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:28.006
I'm glad we didn't ask those questions.

00:27:28.006 --> 00:27:30.430
We're very fortunate
that we got out with it

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:31.780
with only minor injuries.

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:35.885
But yeah, we were completely
on board for all this stuff.

00:27:35.885 --> 00:27:38.797
Many times when we would
go out and chase cows,

00:27:38.797 --> 00:27:42.793
he would be there chasing
cows right along with us.

00:27:42.793 --> 00:27:43.730
Yeah.

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:46.183
There's, we told those stories before.

00:27:48.379 --> 00:27:49.540
He had a trampoline.

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:51.300
And he was very,

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:55.470
he was so like, skinny,

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:57.430
but he was pretty athletic--

00:27:57.430 --> 00:27:58.734
He was very athletic.

00:27:58.734 --> 00:27:59.567
He was very daring.

00:27:59.567 --> 00:28:02.250
And he would do all of
these flips and stuff

00:28:02.250 --> 00:28:03.970
on the trampoline,

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:08.224
and he made up this terminology where,

00:28:08.224 --> 00:28:10.310
I guess all kids do
this on the trampoline,

00:28:10.310 --> 00:28:12.560
where you jump really high, and then you,

00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:13.510
when your in the air,

00:28:13.510 --> 00:28:16.160
you pivot, and then you land on your back,

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:17.980
and you bounce off of your back

00:28:17.980 --> 00:28:19.530
and come back up.

00:28:19.530 --> 00:28:21.620
But he called this a blurah!

00:28:21.620 --> 00:28:22.453
Blurah, yeah.

00:28:22.453 --> 00:28:24.400
Because I think that's the sound he made

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:27.600
when all of the air was
forced out of his lungs

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:30.630
as he impacted with
the trampoline, blurah!

00:28:31.610 --> 00:28:36.610
So he, he had a way of
contextualizing and inventing ways

00:28:37.810 --> 00:28:39.960
to think about things
that all kids were doing

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:41.600
that elevated it.

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:43.300
So it wasn't just let's,

00:28:43.300 --> 00:28:44.840
come over, let's jump on the trampoline.

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:47.400
It's like no let's master
master the art of a blurah.

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:49.270
Well, and I don't know if you,

00:28:49.270 --> 00:28:50.874
I assume you were here for some of this.

00:28:50.874 --> 00:28:53.930
We spend a lot, once
the trampoline happened,

00:28:53.930 --> 00:28:55.710
we spent a lot of time on the trampoline,

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:57.790
like days just on the trampoline,

00:28:57.790 --> 00:28:58.930
figuring out things,

00:28:58.930 --> 00:29:00.070
coming up with moves.

00:29:00.070 --> 00:29:01.720
But one of the things he also did

00:29:03.264 --> 00:29:05.840
is put a tarp

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:09.210
over the trampoline,

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:11.110
and then get dish soap

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:13.160
and put it all over the tarp,

00:29:13.160 --> 00:29:15.393
and then get a hose and wet it down.

00:29:15.393 --> 00:29:19.107
And then the trampoline
was at a slight angle.

00:29:19.107 --> 00:29:21.120
Which is very safe.

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:22.303
And we would run,

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:24.620
dive onto the trampoline,

00:29:24.620 --> 00:29:26.350
slide completely off of it,

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:28.790
like slide completely over it

00:29:28.790 --> 00:29:31.160
and then off the other
side into the woods.

00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:32.070
I remember this.

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:33.280
It was one of those trampolines

00:29:33.280 --> 00:29:34.848
where the springs were exposed too,

00:29:34.848 --> 00:29:36.481
if I remember correctly.
Oh yeah.

00:29:36.481 --> 00:29:38.658
We didn't have a net or anything.

00:29:38.658 --> 00:29:40.910
I don't know how you went
across the springs, but you did.

00:29:40.910 --> 00:29:42.580
No, well, because they
were covered with a tarp.

00:29:42.580 --> 00:29:44.060
That's right.

00:29:44.060 --> 00:29:46.076
The whole thing was covered with a tarp

00:29:46.076 --> 00:29:47.770
and it didn't help that you
kind of sort of hit the bar

00:29:47.770 --> 00:29:49.063
and the springs on the other side.

00:29:49.063 --> 00:29:51.950
But there was many, we get
we went through this phase,

00:29:51.950 --> 00:29:53.340
did you ever spend the
night with us out there

00:29:53.340 --> 00:29:54.830
on the trampoline?

00:29:54.830 --> 00:29:55.663
Uh uh.

00:29:55.663 --> 00:29:56.496
We would,

00:29:57.340 --> 00:29:58.570
we went through,

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:00.440
we stopped like using the A frame

00:30:00.440 --> 00:30:02.493
because it like kind of,
the grass kind of grew up.

00:30:02.493 --> 00:30:05.797
And we just put our sleeping
bags on the trampoline.

00:30:05.797 --> 00:30:08.630
And it was kind of funny because like,

00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:10.660
when you put your sleeping
bags on the trampoline,

00:30:10.660 --> 00:30:13.410
even though you're small, I wasn't small,

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:15.615
but you're relatively light boys.

00:30:15.615 --> 00:30:19.030
You know, the nature of
a trampoline is it dips--

00:30:19.030 --> 00:30:20.153
It sags.

00:30:20.153 --> 00:30:21.199
In the middle and so,

00:30:21.199 --> 00:30:22.032
by the middle of the night,

00:30:22.032 --> 00:30:23.160
you're both kinda like
right up against each other

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:24.910
in your sleeping bags.

00:30:24.910 --> 00:30:29.060
And the thing I, one of the
things I remember about Ben

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:32.300
is his sense of humor was so silly.

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:33.133
Yeah.

00:30:33.133 --> 00:30:34.310
Like it was just this,

00:30:34.310 --> 00:30:35.680
we would sit out there

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:36.950
and we would tell,

00:30:36.950 --> 00:30:38.570
we would tell each other jokes.

00:30:38.570 --> 00:30:40.640
It was make up jokes.

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:41.932
Yeah.

00:30:41.932 --> 00:30:42.765
And they were always

00:30:43.618 --> 00:30:47.010
horrible jokes

00:30:47.010 --> 00:30:48.870
that like didn't have punchlines.

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:50.550
It was very anti-comedy.

00:30:50.550 --> 00:30:52.750
But you would achieve
some sort of delirium.

00:30:52.750 --> 00:30:54.400
Oh, we would start laughing.

00:30:54.400 --> 00:30:55.237
Yeah.

00:30:55.237 --> 00:30:57.470
Just so uncontrollably.

00:30:57.470 --> 00:30:58.303
Couldn't stop.

00:30:58.303 --> 00:31:00.620
Having those like laugh attacks

00:31:00.620 --> 00:31:01.680
where you would just, you can't stop.

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:03.293
You're like what,

00:31:03.293 --> 00:31:04.126
I don't remember what you said.

00:31:04.126 --> 00:31:05.794
I don't remember, your
joke probably wasn't good.

00:31:05.794 --> 00:31:06.800
Yeah.

00:31:06.800 --> 00:31:08.103
I don't remember it now.

00:31:08.103 --> 00:31:10.350
He was funny though.

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:12.240
And he, you know, he watched Letterman.

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:13.480
He watched Carson.

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:16.720
He would stay up late and do that.

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:18.263
And that's just something that I just,

00:31:18.263 --> 00:31:19.610
I wasn't allowed to do.

00:31:19.610 --> 00:31:21.860
And I didn't, it was,

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:23.330
it was scary to stay up late.

00:31:23.330 --> 00:31:24.430
I'm not gonna do that.

00:31:25.785 --> 00:31:27.678
But yeah, so,

00:31:27.678 --> 00:31:31.544
and I do remember he
went through this spell.

00:31:31.544 --> 00:31:33.570
I mean people talk about the innuendo

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:35.210
of the good morning.

00:31:35.210 --> 00:31:37.080
Like he was the king of that.

00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:37.913
Oh yeah.

00:31:37.913 --> 00:31:39.110
I remember like in the,

00:31:40.190 --> 00:31:41.510
in grade school,

00:31:41.510 --> 00:31:44.193
the kids, I mean the
teachers would say something,

00:31:44.193 --> 00:31:48.490
and all right, so and so,

00:31:48.490 --> 00:31:49.730
you need to come up to the front.

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:50.840
And he would just start laughing.

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:52.360
And he would like look at us and like,

00:31:52.360 --> 00:31:54.740
he, I remember he had this like,

00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:56.440
he had a dirty mind.

00:31:56.440 --> 00:31:57.280
And he would like,

00:31:57.280 --> 00:31:58.840
he would love laughing at things

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:00.668
that were like forced innuendos.

00:32:00.668 --> 00:32:01.501
Yeah.

00:32:01.501 --> 00:32:02.730
And we went through this

00:32:02.730 --> 00:32:07.287
season of really reveling in the innuendo.

00:32:08.710 --> 00:32:09.543
Do you remember that?

00:32:09.543 --> 00:32:12.120
I well, I still revel in the innuendo.

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:12.953
You still do.

00:32:12.953 --> 00:32:14.036
It wasn't a stage.

00:32:14.036 --> 00:32:15.697
It continued throughout my entire life.

00:32:15.697 --> 00:32:17.100
And he was such a smart guy.

00:32:17.100 --> 00:32:19.820
I mean I think he did
end up reading a lot.

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:22.681
And he had that computer

00:32:22.681 --> 00:32:25.673
in the back of the house there.

00:32:25.673 --> 00:32:28.930
And again, I come over there one day,

00:32:28.930 --> 00:32:30.830
and the two of you had been working on

00:32:32.360 --> 00:32:36.230
a from scratch computer
coded role playing game

00:32:36.230 --> 00:32:37.350
that you sat me down,

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:39.462
and I was like the first user

00:32:39.462 --> 00:32:42.430
to just like test out
your role playing game.

00:32:42.430 --> 00:32:44.170
It was only text based.

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:45.003
Well and--
And you had spent

00:32:45.003 --> 00:32:46.628
hours on this.

00:32:46.628 --> 00:32:47.461
Well, we had spent--
Days.

00:32:47.461 --> 00:32:48.677
We'd spent,

00:32:48.677 --> 00:32:49.709
we would revisit it.

00:32:49.709 --> 00:32:50.920
It was called the Isle of Rhettben.

00:32:50.920 --> 00:32:51.753
Okay.

00:32:51.753 --> 00:32:52.586
Which was not very creative.

00:32:52.586 --> 00:32:54.023
We just combined our two names.

00:32:55.032 --> 00:32:55.865
I see that.

00:32:55.865 --> 00:32:56.698
It was,

00:32:58.180 --> 00:32:59.990
you know, it was just basic programming.

00:32:59.990 --> 00:33:02.140
And again, I don't know how
Ben learned how to do this,

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:05.000
but he, I remember sitting
at his house one time,

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:09.193
and once I kinda understood
basic programming,

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:12.440
I remember he just went
to sleep one night,

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:14.340
and I stayed up like almost all night

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:16.610
just trudging away at like,

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:18.020
it was a choose your own adventure game.

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:18.853
Yeah.
So it's like you

00:33:18.853 --> 00:33:20.500
had to create these trees,

00:33:20.500 --> 00:33:21.590
these decision trees.

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:22.570
And it was like all right,

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:26.850
if Rhettben decides that he's
going to kill this dragon,

00:33:26.850 --> 00:33:28.420
then this is gotta happen.

00:33:28.420 --> 00:33:30.820
And I think if you actually
looked at the layering

00:33:30.820 --> 00:33:32.204
of this thing, it probably only had like

00:33:32.204 --> 00:33:34.506
four decision points.

00:33:34.506 --> 00:33:37.740
But that creates, I don't
know how to do that math

00:33:37.740 --> 00:33:38.573
off the top of my head,

00:33:38.573 --> 00:33:40.408
but it creates a number
of potential outcomes.

00:33:40.408 --> 00:33:42.550
And some where you would die,

00:33:42.550 --> 00:33:44.023
and there was some where
you would keep going.

00:33:44.023 --> 00:33:46.827
And do you remember
what happens if you win?

00:33:46.827 --> 00:33:48.899
Because it was a color monitor.

00:33:48.899 --> 00:33:49.949
No.

00:33:49.949 --> 00:33:52.362
The screen would flash

00:33:52.362 --> 00:33:57.362
basically like 12 different
colors over and over again.

00:33:58.200 --> 00:34:00.901
And I think that it played some tones

00:34:00.901 --> 00:34:04.900
that was like some
little celebration music.

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:06.656
Just like every other
video game of that age?

00:34:06.656 --> 00:34:08.873
I never got to the end.

00:34:09.770 --> 00:34:10.920
Yeah, exactly.

00:34:10.920 --> 00:34:11.753
But like--

00:34:11.753 --> 00:34:13.500
I've never beaten one video game.

00:34:13.500 --> 00:34:14.600
And again, and then that,

00:34:14.600 --> 00:34:17.030
that turned into like,
once I understood that,

00:34:17.030 --> 00:34:20.030
thanks to Ben, is when I
ended up writing that game

00:34:20.030 --> 00:34:22.340
on the calculator that was the
Adventure of Meryl Haggard.

00:34:22.340 --> 00:34:23.173
Yeah.

00:34:23.173 --> 00:34:24.006
I just wrote my senior year

00:34:24.006 --> 00:34:25.731
that you had to go into
prison and get Meryl.

00:34:25.731 --> 00:34:28.260
I would've never done any of that thing

00:34:28.260 --> 00:34:29.430
because the thing that Ben,

00:34:29.430 --> 00:34:31.918
we had, kinda what I
was getting at before--

00:34:31.918 --> 00:34:33.030
You had to break Meryl outta prison.

00:34:33.030 --> 00:34:35.864
We had an appetite for this kind of thing.

00:34:35.864 --> 00:34:38.290
Once you kind of gave us the opportunity--

00:34:38.290 --> 00:34:39.123
You had the avenue.

00:34:39.123 --> 00:34:39.956
You took it.

00:34:39.956 --> 00:34:41.091
Yeah.

00:34:41.091 --> 00:34:41.924
But he had the knowledge.

00:34:41.924 --> 00:34:42.981
Yeah.

00:34:42.981 --> 00:34:43.814
I think that's the thing that Ben had.

00:34:43.814 --> 00:34:46.710
He didn't just have the appetite for it,

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:47.570
he had the ability.

00:34:47.570 --> 00:34:49.200
He would find the way to do it,

00:34:49.200 --> 00:34:50.033
and we were just like,

00:34:50.033 --> 00:34:51.560
ah, we got this guy that
knows how to do this stuff.

00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:53.840
So he's just gonna tell us

00:34:53.840 --> 00:34:55.190
what the next thing that we're doing.

00:34:55.190 --> 00:34:56.193
He was uninhibited.

00:34:56.193 --> 00:34:58.390
Sometimes we would go over there

00:34:58.390 --> 00:35:01.116
and we were all into rap music.

00:35:01.116 --> 00:35:05.523
And he would find these
interesting rap songs

00:35:05.523 --> 00:35:09.330
that weren't so mainstream.

00:35:09.330 --> 00:35:12.369
Like listen to this Fu Schnicken song.

00:35:12.369 --> 00:35:15.095
And I developed this dance,

00:35:15.095 --> 00:35:17.730
and then he would then go into this--

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:18.650
He would teach us dances.

00:35:18.650 --> 00:35:20.260
It's so crazy.

00:35:20.260 --> 00:35:21.730
I would call it a jig.

00:35:21.730 --> 00:35:24.610
It was like mostly in the hips,

00:35:24.610 --> 00:35:26.560
knees and ankles.

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:27.821
Well, it was--

00:35:27.821 --> 00:35:28.654
This particular dance,

00:35:28.654 --> 00:35:30.210
and he would do these dance things

00:35:30.210 --> 00:35:32.760
that looked like, kinda looked like

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:35.650
what Will Ferrell did as Elf

00:35:35.650 --> 00:35:37.948
in the basement of the publisher.

00:35:37.948 --> 00:35:39.900
And then the three of us would sit there

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:41.095
and try to figure out--

00:35:41.095 --> 00:35:41.928
We would learn the dance.

00:35:41.928 --> 00:35:42.761
Do you remember the
time we went over there

00:35:42.761 --> 00:35:43.840
and he was like,

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:45.317
I figured out how to do the Roger Rabbit.

00:35:45.317 --> 00:35:46.320
Yeah.

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:49.270
And the Roger Rabbit is when you put your,

00:35:49.270 --> 00:35:51.370
well at least, we may have been wrong.

00:35:51.370 --> 00:35:53.770
But it was when you
basically put your feet

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:54.603
behind each other

00:35:54.603 --> 00:35:55.869
and you're like doing this thing,

00:35:55.869 --> 00:35:56.840
and you're like, you're stepping.

00:35:56.840 --> 00:35:58.330
And it's not easy to do.

00:35:58.330 --> 00:35:59.163
Yeah.

00:35:59.163 --> 00:36:00.325
And like we sat there,

00:36:00.325 --> 00:36:02.774
and go out in the driveway and like,

00:36:02.774 --> 00:36:04.820
trying to get this dance down.

00:36:04.820 --> 00:36:06.065
I mean when you're in
middle school, it's like,

00:36:06.065 --> 00:36:09.760
you have, if you're not uninhibited,

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:11.753
you're not gonna sit
there and invent a dance.

00:36:11.753 --> 00:36:14.150
And if you are, you're not necessarily

00:36:14.150 --> 00:36:15.590
gonna invite your boys over

00:36:15.590 --> 00:36:16.996
to like teach 'em.

00:36:16.996 --> 00:36:18.565
This is something the girls did

00:36:18.565 --> 00:36:20.870
like when they, like
the cheerleader girls.

00:36:20.870 --> 00:36:22.590
They would get together and
do their cheerleading dances.

00:36:22.590 --> 00:36:24.476
But isn't it ironically
Tic Tok changing that.

00:36:24.476 --> 00:36:25.810
Yeah, definitely.

00:36:25.810 --> 00:36:27.680
But aren't people like teaching
each other how to dance.

00:36:27.680 --> 00:36:29.110
Oh he would kill at Tic Tok, man.

00:36:29.110 --> 00:36:30.494
He would've been a Tic
Toker extraordinaire.

00:36:30.494 --> 00:36:31.952
He would've been a,

00:36:31.952 --> 00:36:33.974
he would've been amazing at it.

00:36:33.974 --> 00:36:38.010
Yeah, music was a big part
of what we were doing.

00:36:38.010 --> 00:36:42.450
And he's the one who then
bought a drum machine.

00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:46.400
Now we had already done some
talent show performances,

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:48.048
as we've talked about before.

00:36:48.048 --> 00:36:50.832
We would, Link and I would kind of--

00:36:50.832 --> 00:36:54.170
The story I'm gonna tell is
the seventh grade talent show.

00:36:54.170 --> 00:36:55.003
Right.

00:36:55.003 --> 00:36:58.590
But what we would do
before the drum machine

00:36:58.590 --> 00:37:00.033
is we would sing along with a track.

00:37:00.033 --> 00:37:01.300
Yeah.

00:37:01.300 --> 00:37:02.610
And so we would just be, we would play

00:37:02.610 --> 00:37:04.170
like a Digital Underground
song or whatever

00:37:04.170 --> 00:37:05.780
and we would sing it.

00:37:05.780 --> 00:37:08.949
But then Ben gets this like Yamaha,

00:37:08.949 --> 00:37:11.410
I don't remember what brand it was,

00:37:11.410 --> 00:37:13.399
drum machine, which was basically this,

00:37:13.399 --> 00:37:16.751
you know, black drum machine.

00:37:16.751 --> 00:37:17.976
I remember four--

00:37:17.976 --> 00:37:19.150
Yeah, pads.

00:37:19.150 --> 00:37:20.080
Pads.

00:37:20.080 --> 00:37:21.290
He played--
A bunch of things,

00:37:21.290 --> 00:37:22.123
you could play the drums.

00:37:22.123 --> 00:37:24.513
But then you could also kind of program

00:37:24.513 --> 00:37:27.140
some beats, it had some
built in beats as well.

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:28.943
He could also beatbox.

00:37:28.943 --> 00:37:30.909
Yep.

00:37:30.909 --> 00:37:31.920
Better than us.

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:32.753
But we would all do it.

00:37:32.753 --> 00:37:35.134
It's something that we
did in our spare time.

00:37:35.134 --> 00:37:38.939
I remember, seventh grade,

00:37:38.939 --> 00:37:41.110
I got the job of being the guy

00:37:41.110 --> 00:37:42.840
who set up the microphones.

00:37:42.840 --> 00:37:45.000
And Ben also got the job.

00:37:45.000 --> 00:37:46.230
We worked together.

00:37:46.230 --> 00:37:47.800
Whenever there was an assembly

00:37:47.800 --> 00:37:49.040
at Buies Creek Elementary School,

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:50.901
we would go and we would,

00:37:50.901 --> 00:37:53.270
we'd get out of class 15 minutes early

00:37:53.270 --> 00:37:55.960
because we had to set up the microphones

00:37:55.960 --> 00:37:57.370
and test them and make sure they worked

00:37:57.370 --> 00:37:59.346
before whatever the assembly was.

00:37:59.346 --> 00:38:02.260
And so we spent a lot
of time setting them up.

00:38:02.260 --> 00:38:05.120
And then when the talent
show rolled around,

00:38:05.120 --> 00:38:06.956
and there was like rehearsals for that,

00:38:06.956 --> 00:38:10.120
we would hang out and
setting up the microphones

00:38:10.120 --> 00:38:11.290
for the rehearsal,

00:38:11.290 --> 00:38:13.000
and as seventh graders,

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:15.647
it was our first year to
be able to participate

00:38:15.647 --> 00:38:17.000
in this talent show,

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:21.470
which we worshiped the
seventh and eighth graders

00:38:21.470 --> 00:38:25.842
who would perform an amazing
rap song the previous years.

00:38:25.842 --> 00:38:29.630
So we finally, yeah,
we performed same song

00:38:29.630 --> 00:38:30.863
by Digital Underground.

00:38:30.863 --> 00:38:34.360
And then Ben was a part of that.

00:38:34.360 --> 00:38:36.010
And I remember we were
setting up the microphones,

00:38:36.010 --> 00:38:37.960
and then we, we'd either,

00:38:37.960 --> 00:38:40.523
we were about to or we
had just done a rehearsal,

00:38:42.859 --> 00:38:44.700
and we go backstage.

00:38:44.700 --> 00:38:46.077
Well, I go backstage and I,

00:38:46.077 --> 00:38:49.773
I notice that Ben is crying.

00:38:51.110 --> 00:38:54.111
I don't know that I'd ever
seen Ben cry, you know.

00:38:54.111 --> 00:38:56.322
And I was like hey, hey man,

00:38:56.322 --> 00:39:00.470
it's okay, we're gonna, you
know, we're gonna do great.

00:39:00.470 --> 00:39:01.303
We're gonna kill it.

00:39:01.303 --> 00:39:02.700
We're gonna kill it at the talent show,

00:39:02.700 --> 00:39:05.520
like we're, I'm feeling good about this.

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:06.900
The first graders are gonna love us.

00:39:06.900 --> 00:39:08.120
We know the words.

00:39:08.120 --> 00:39:10.010
Your beatboxing is amazing.

00:39:10.010 --> 00:39:13.172
And this drum machine makes
you look totally legit, man.

00:39:13.172 --> 00:39:15.884
And he said that's not it.

00:39:15.884 --> 00:39:20.820
And he said, I said well
what's, what's wrong.

00:39:20.820 --> 00:39:23.088
And he said, oh, don't worry about it.

00:39:23.088 --> 00:39:26.220
And he didn't tell me what was wrong.

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:27.430
But then I remember

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:31.570
you told me

00:39:32.810 --> 00:39:37.010
days after this that your
mom had given you the news

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:39.210
after talking to Ben's mom

00:39:39.210 --> 00:39:40.863
that he was sick.

00:39:42.160 --> 00:39:45.882
And we're like wow guys, he's sick.

00:39:45.882 --> 00:39:47.955
And we had seen--
He's sick?

00:39:47.955 --> 00:39:49.980
We had noticed that Ben would,

00:39:49.980 --> 00:39:51.619
you know, miss a day of school.

00:39:51.619 --> 00:39:52.879
Yeah.

00:39:52.879 --> 00:39:56.560
But that wasn't that unusual.

00:39:56.560 --> 00:39:57.930
But it was happening.

00:39:57.930 --> 00:39:59.810
It was more frequent than you would.

00:39:59.810 --> 00:40:01.109
Yeah, it got increasingly frequent.

00:40:01.109 --> 00:40:02.480
Expect.

00:40:02.480 --> 00:40:06.330
And it's not anything that
he talked about, you know.

00:40:06.330 --> 00:40:10.373
He didn't, he didn't wanna bring it up.

00:40:10.373 --> 00:40:12.022
But as it turns out,

00:40:12.022 --> 00:40:13.940
he eventually would tell us

00:40:13.940 --> 00:40:16.600
that the doctor's diagnosed him

00:40:16.600 --> 00:40:19.183
with chronic fatigue syndrome.

00:40:19.183 --> 00:40:20.890
Which at the time,

00:40:20.890 --> 00:40:22.523
I mean it's still a very,

00:40:24.410 --> 00:40:26.620
they still have, last I checked,

00:40:26.620 --> 00:40:28.010
they don't have a whole
lot of understanding

00:40:28.010 --> 00:40:29.520
about this disease.

00:40:29.520 --> 00:40:31.666
It's sort of like one of those,

00:40:31.666 --> 00:40:33.953
you know, like Lyme Disease used to be.

00:40:33.953 --> 00:40:36.620
Like I don't know, we don't
know exactly what this is,

00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:38.530
and this might just be a name

00:40:38.530 --> 00:40:40.347
for a family of different
conditions or whatever.

00:40:40.347 --> 00:40:43.300
But this was the beginning
of people talking about it

00:40:43.300 --> 00:40:45.129
in the mid-90s.

00:40:45.129 --> 00:40:46.050
And it--

00:40:46.050 --> 00:40:47.280
Or actually the early 90s.

00:40:47.280 --> 00:40:48.847
And it was, it was also discussed--

00:40:48.847 --> 00:40:50.250
We had no idea what was going on.

00:40:50.250 --> 00:40:51.350
It was discussed at times

00:40:51.350 --> 00:40:53.982
of kinda being a catch-all

00:40:53.982 --> 00:40:55.850
for when you don't know exactly

00:40:55.850 --> 00:40:57.370
what's wrong with someone too.

00:40:57.370 --> 00:40:59.267
But you know that they're
extremely fatigued.

00:40:59.267 --> 00:41:01.891
I mean he described some mornings

00:41:01.891 --> 00:41:04.820
not being able to get out of bed.

00:41:04.820 --> 00:41:07.151
I do remember him telling us at one point

00:41:07.151 --> 00:41:09.394
in seventh grade, he said,

00:41:09.394 --> 00:41:14.071
sometimes he could barely
crawl to the bathroom

00:41:14.071 --> 00:41:15.698
in order to use the bathroom.

00:41:15.698 --> 00:41:17.596
We couldn't believe it.

00:41:17.596 --> 00:41:19.133
We didn't know how to process it.

00:41:19.133 --> 00:41:21.710
We never saw this.

00:41:21.710 --> 00:41:23.070
Like we never saw it firsthand.

00:41:23.070 --> 00:41:25.600
He did, he didn't look well.

00:41:25.600 --> 00:41:29.133
He started to look more frail.

00:41:30.650 --> 00:41:33.520
But when we would call him

00:41:33.520 --> 00:41:34.980
and say hey, do you wanna do something,

00:41:34.980 --> 00:41:36.280
sometimes the answer would be no,

00:41:36.280 --> 00:41:37.530
but if the answer was yes,

00:41:37.530 --> 00:41:40.299
it would be, we would be
able to go on our adventures.

00:41:40.299 --> 00:41:41.648
Yeah, we'd show up

00:41:41.648 --> 00:41:43.890
and we'd hop on our bikes,

00:41:43.890 --> 00:41:46.523
and we'd go play just like we always had.

00:41:46.523 --> 00:41:49.980
In eighth grade, then
that was seventh grade,

00:41:49.980 --> 00:41:53.442
in eighth grade, when school started back,

00:41:53.442 --> 00:41:56.075
he didn't come back.

00:41:56.075 --> 00:41:57.495
Right, they said that he

00:41:57.495 --> 00:41:59.339
was going to be basically homeschooled.

00:41:59.339 --> 00:42:00.172
Yeah.

00:42:00.172 --> 00:42:01.330
From that point on.

00:42:01.330 --> 00:42:04.006
And I remember, you know, this was,

00:42:04.006 --> 00:42:08.110
I've never been good at processing
this type of information.

00:42:08.110 --> 00:42:09.730
I'm still not good at it.

00:42:09.730 --> 00:42:11.310
Like when somebody's
going through something,

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:14.498
I'm like I don't know how
to comfort you right now.

00:42:14.498 --> 00:42:18.180
It's like that part of my
brain was damaged or something.

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:21.200
You know, it's like, and I remember

00:42:24.840 --> 00:42:26.200
being like this is this guy

00:42:26.200 --> 00:42:27.930
that we've spent all this time with,

00:42:27.930 --> 00:42:30.270
we've done all this stuff with,

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:32.762
and all of a sudden, it's like he's not,

00:42:32.762 --> 00:42:34.270
oh, he's not at school.

00:42:34.270 --> 00:42:36.520
And he's sick.

00:42:36.520 --> 00:42:41.367
How could, how could
someone like as so vibrant,

00:42:41.367 --> 00:42:45.817
that has so much zest for life,

00:42:45.817 --> 00:42:50.633
how could they be the
ones drained of life?

00:42:50.633 --> 00:42:53.598
It just, it did not add up.

00:42:53.598 --> 00:42:54.977
Well there was something else--

00:42:54.977 --> 00:42:55.910
I couldn't believe it.

00:42:55.910 --> 00:42:57.916
Well, the interesting
thing that was happening

00:42:57.916 --> 00:43:01.564
as well, and this is like
a tale as old as time,

00:43:01.564 --> 00:43:04.360
is that we were kind of,

00:43:04.360 --> 00:43:07.059
we were kind of moving along with

00:43:07.059 --> 00:43:11.008
what it was like to be in middle school.

00:43:11.008 --> 00:43:12.770
And we thought I was, I had a girlfriend.

00:43:12.770 --> 00:43:13.980
Yeah, I got a girlfriend.

00:43:13.980 --> 00:43:16.380
And it was like that for a while.

00:43:16.380 --> 00:43:17.605
That was the only thing that mattered.

00:43:17.605 --> 00:43:18.740
Well that was definitely your world.

00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:19.573
Yeah.

00:43:19.573 --> 00:43:20.406
Whenever you got a girlfriend.

00:43:20.406 --> 00:43:21.507
Yeah.

00:43:21.507 --> 00:43:22.454
And so it was like, okay, I'm playing,

00:43:22.454 --> 00:43:23.860
I'm playing basketball.

00:43:23.860 --> 00:43:25.153
I'm thinking about high school.

00:43:25.153 --> 00:43:27.536
I'm thinking about all
the girls in high school.

00:43:27.536 --> 00:43:29.434
I got my girlfriend right now.

00:43:29.434 --> 00:43:32.516
We were doing those sort
of middle school things.

00:43:32.516 --> 00:43:35.930
And it wasn't that the, you know,

00:43:35.930 --> 00:43:38.307
going into the river and
fishing and swinging on vines

00:43:38.307 --> 00:43:40.163
and camping and all this
stuff wasn't something

00:43:40.163 --> 00:43:41.760
that we wanted to keep doing.

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:44.297
But it had, it wasn't
everything to us anymore.

00:43:44.297 --> 00:43:47.456
It was something that
we did when we could.

00:43:47.456 --> 00:43:48.460
Right.

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:50.610
And so because we couldn't,

00:43:50.610 --> 00:43:55.230
and it was interesting
because I, you know,

00:43:55.230 --> 00:43:56.707
I felt like that

00:43:56.707 --> 00:43:58.585
because a lot of you have
asked the question like

00:43:58.585 --> 00:44:00.969
you and Link been best friends,

00:44:00.969 --> 00:44:03.200
you've been best friends
this whole time, right?

00:44:03.200 --> 00:44:05.229
It's like, yeah, we we
have been best friends.

00:44:05.229 --> 00:44:07.817
But it wasn't like it was
me and you and nobody else.

00:44:07.817 --> 00:44:08.650
Yeah.

00:44:08.650 --> 00:44:10.723
In a lot of ways, me and Ben were closer

00:44:10.723 --> 00:44:13.009
for a few of those years,

00:44:13.009 --> 00:44:15.294
like in like, third, fourth, fifth grade.

00:44:15.294 --> 00:44:19.429
And then as he started kind
of dealing with his stuff,

00:44:19.429 --> 00:44:22.280
and me and you kind of continued on

00:44:22.280 --> 00:44:24.110
living a quote unquote, normal life

00:44:24.110 --> 00:44:26.210
that was kind of unencumbered

00:44:26.210 --> 00:44:28.590
by the kind of issues that he was facing,

00:44:28.590 --> 00:44:30.870
then we basically like our friendship

00:44:30.870 --> 00:44:32.730
kind of just superseded
every other friendship,

00:44:32.730 --> 00:44:33.980
and it's been that way ever since.

00:44:33.980 --> 00:44:35.447
Yeah, I mean, I definitely remember

00:44:35.447 --> 00:44:39.840
feeling on the outskirts of the trio.

00:44:39.840 --> 00:44:41.700
You know, it was like I said,

00:44:41.700 --> 00:44:42.920
I would show up to things

00:44:42.920 --> 00:44:47.920
and kind of have to, I would
gather what I missed, you know.

00:44:48.080 --> 00:44:52.403
I think it's fair to say that
for for that period of time,

00:44:54.190 --> 00:44:56.900
we were bests, I don't know,

00:44:56.900 --> 00:44:58.600
I think, I think we were best friends,

00:44:58.600 --> 00:45:01.330
but you were bester
friend, the two of you.

00:45:01.330 --> 00:45:02.827
Yeah, it wasn't something
we thought about.

00:45:02.827 --> 00:45:03.660
Yeah.

00:45:03.660 --> 00:45:05.890
And there were no list being made.

00:45:05.890 --> 00:45:06.723
Right.

00:45:06.723 --> 00:45:08.240
And there was no discussions about that.

00:45:08.240 --> 00:45:11.680
But yeah, I, I could
feel that a little bit.

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:16.100
But again, it was because I,

00:45:16.100 --> 00:45:18.160
you know, I knew it wasn't calculated.

00:45:18.160 --> 00:45:23.160
Ben was the, the kindest,
most welcoming person of all,

00:45:23.206 --> 00:45:27.280
anyone we had met at that age, you know.

00:45:27.280 --> 00:45:29.743
There's, it was impossible
to hold anything against him.

00:45:29.743 --> 00:45:32.410
So I didn't feel jealous.

00:45:32.410 --> 00:45:36.030
Like I said, I felt like, I
felt privileged to be invited

00:45:36.030 --> 00:45:39.070
as this scared kid who wasn't adventurous,

00:45:39.070 --> 00:45:40.830
that was kind of brought
along for the ride

00:45:40.830 --> 00:45:45.280
and kind of ushered out
of my shell, so to speak.

00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:48.270
But you know, it was difficult living

00:45:48.270 --> 00:45:49.444
on the other side of Buies Creek

00:45:49.444 --> 00:45:53.008
and knowing that there was
probably stuff going on.

00:45:53.008 --> 00:45:55.380
There were A frames being built,

00:45:55.380 --> 00:45:58.380
and there were, there were
fish being caught and you know,

00:45:58.380 --> 00:45:59.720
I wasn't a part of it.

00:45:59.720 --> 00:46:01.440
But as we got older,

00:46:01.440 --> 00:46:02.970
I was increasingly a part of it.

00:46:02.970 --> 00:46:06.302
And then as Ben started,
wasn't able to do as much

00:46:06.302 --> 00:46:11.302
then I think we got
even closer and closer.

00:46:16.366 --> 00:46:17.199
So kinda fast forward a little bit.

00:46:17.199 --> 00:46:20.330
So high school, high school got you know,

00:46:20.330 --> 00:46:21.720
I have a lot of regrets

00:46:21.720 --> 00:46:23.650
about the amount of time that would pass

00:46:23.650 --> 00:46:25.060
between seeing Ben.

00:46:25.060 --> 00:46:25.893
Yeah.

00:46:26.750 --> 00:46:29.970
And I would hear, my mom,
like, would talk to his mom

00:46:29.970 --> 00:46:31.610
and would find out that like,

00:46:31.610 --> 00:46:34.580
oh, Ben is really struggling
with chronic fatigue.

00:46:34.580 --> 00:46:35.910
And, you know,

00:46:35.910 --> 00:46:38.410
he basically didn't get out
of bed this week or whatever.

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:39.786
And then she would be like,

00:46:39.786 --> 00:46:41.891
you should, you need to go see him.

00:46:41.891 --> 00:46:44.410
And it was interesting because,

00:46:44.410 --> 00:46:45.763
because, again, I'm,

00:46:46.780 --> 00:46:48.529
I'm not making this, I'm
not making an excuse.

00:46:48.529 --> 00:46:51.550
I felt so inadequate

00:46:54.706 --> 00:46:55.539
to engage and be like,

00:46:55.539 --> 00:46:57.261
I know we're not gonna
go over there and like,

00:46:57.261 --> 00:46:58.094
go do something fun.

00:46:58.094 --> 00:46:58.927
I'm gonna sit there

00:46:58.927 --> 00:47:00.449
and we're going to talk about
what he's what he's doing.

00:47:00.449 --> 00:47:01.619
Like am I supposed to
sit beside his bedside?

00:47:01.619 --> 00:47:03.355
I don't, Ben,

00:47:03.355 --> 00:47:06.348
Ben game the impression he didn't want us

00:47:06.348 --> 00:47:09.930
to see him that way.

00:47:09.930 --> 00:47:10.763
Yeah, right.

00:47:10.763 --> 00:47:12.350
And, you know,

00:47:12.350 --> 00:47:16.099
I also regret not, not saying
you know what, it's fine.

00:47:16.099 --> 00:47:18.780
You know, I want to hang out with you

00:47:18.780 --> 00:47:21.162
even if that just means
sitting in your room.

00:47:21.162 --> 00:47:24.529
But we didn't really have a,

00:47:24.529 --> 00:47:28.310
I don't know we didn't
have a track to go on.

00:47:28.310 --> 00:47:30.010
It's like, okay, this is how you do it.

00:47:30.010 --> 00:47:31.663
This is how you empathize with somebody.

00:47:31.663 --> 00:47:33.889
You know, I do wish you,

00:47:33.889 --> 00:47:35.610
to your parents credit,

00:47:35.610 --> 00:47:36.680
to your mom's credit, she was like,

00:47:36.680 --> 00:47:38.433
hey, you need to, you need to go see him.

00:47:38.433 --> 00:47:39.740
And you did.

00:47:39.740 --> 00:47:40.770
Yeah.

00:47:40.770 --> 00:47:42.210
And then I would hear about it.

00:47:42.210 --> 00:47:45.340
Again, I felt a little bit
more on the outskirts of it.

00:47:45.340 --> 00:47:46.621
But it kind of felt--

00:47:46.621 --> 00:47:47.561
It was intimidating.

00:47:47.561 --> 00:47:49.403
But it felt like it was,

00:47:49.403 --> 00:47:51.520
it was like, oh, what are
we going to talk about now?

00:47:51.520 --> 00:47:52.743
Yeah.

00:47:52.743 --> 00:47:55.895
Because I'm doing all these really typical

00:47:55.895 --> 00:47:58.557
high school things, you know.

00:47:58.557 --> 00:48:00.740
It's like what am I
gonna talk to Ben about.

00:48:00.740 --> 00:48:01.640
I'm supposed to talk about

00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:03.180
all the stuff that you can't do.

00:48:03.180 --> 00:48:04.599
Right.

00:48:04.599 --> 00:48:07.780
And so the times between
visiting each other

00:48:07.780 --> 00:48:10.854
would just get longer and longer.

00:48:10.854 --> 00:48:12.000
And then--

00:48:12.000 --> 00:48:13.148
But there would still be times

00:48:13.148 --> 00:48:14.770
throughout high school

00:48:14.770 --> 00:48:17.020
that we would get together.

00:48:17.020 --> 00:48:19.401
I think, am I right about this?

00:48:19.401 --> 00:48:22.110
I remember--

00:48:22.110 --> 00:48:22.943
Sometimes, yeah.

00:48:22.943 --> 00:48:24.970
Ben became famous

00:48:24.970 --> 00:48:28.240
for his running, which was extremely odd

00:48:28.240 --> 00:48:29.620
given everything we've told you.

00:48:29.620 --> 00:48:33.271
He would, he always wore these duck shoes.

00:48:33.271 --> 00:48:35.623
Now a duck boot, you know,

00:48:35.623 --> 00:48:36.830
it's a lace up boot

00:48:36.830 --> 00:48:38.334
that the whole bottom is like rubber,

00:48:38.334 --> 00:48:39.927
so it's waterproof.

00:48:39.927 --> 00:48:41.790
Those were his signature shoes.

00:48:41.790 --> 00:48:43.690
The only type of shoes that
he wore were duck shoes.

00:48:43.690 --> 00:48:46.910
And he wore low top duck shoes

00:48:46.910 --> 00:48:50.040
where it had like three
eyelets on either side,

00:48:50.040 --> 00:48:51.560
or two and you lace it up.

00:48:51.560 --> 00:48:52.876
He would not wear socks.

00:48:52.876 --> 00:48:55.630
And he would wear those duck boots,

00:48:55.630 --> 00:48:57.370
but they were supposed to keep water out,

00:48:57.370 --> 00:48:59.280
but like he would walk through stuff

00:48:59.280 --> 00:49:00.500
and the water would be all,

00:49:00.500 --> 00:49:01.680
going all in it.

00:49:01.680 --> 00:49:02.700
He wouldn't care.

00:49:02.700 --> 00:49:04.650
But he would run

00:49:04.650 --> 00:49:05.810
for miles.

00:49:05.810 --> 00:49:06.643
He would jog around Keith Hills.

00:49:06.643 --> 00:49:08.254
He would jog around Keith Hills

00:49:08.254 --> 00:49:10.933
in these shoes.

00:49:10.933 --> 00:49:14.503
And he was such and odd bird, right.

00:49:14.503 --> 00:49:16.440
Everybody, everybody knew,

00:49:16.440 --> 00:49:17.770
oh, there goes Ben.

00:49:17.770 --> 00:49:19.206
He's got chronic fatigue syndrome, yet

00:49:19.206 --> 00:49:21.500
he's running around.

00:49:21.500 --> 00:49:22.333
You know it's--

00:49:22.333 --> 00:49:24.169
Well, I mean and I don't know, again,

00:49:24.169 --> 00:49:28.768
I don't know, we never talked
a lot about that period

00:49:28.768 --> 00:49:30.652
in his life.

00:49:30.652 --> 00:49:33.820
But my understanding is
that he would have good days

00:49:33.820 --> 00:49:34.690
and bad days.
Yeah.

00:49:34.690 --> 00:49:35.830
And so it's like okay,

00:49:35.830 --> 00:49:36.900
I've got energy today.

00:49:36.900 --> 00:49:38.762
I'm gonna, I'm going out.

00:49:38.762 --> 00:49:39.930
I'm gonna run.

00:49:39.930 --> 00:49:40.885
Yeah.

00:49:40.885 --> 00:49:42.379
And then we would get together.

00:49:42.379 --> 00:49:45.202
It was just increasingly infrequent

00:49:45.202 --> 00:49:48.082
as we got into high school.

00:49:48.082 --> 00:49:51.543
Okay, and one of the other
things that was happening,

00:49:51.543 --> 00:49:54.220
and we talked a little bit about this

00:49:54.220 --> 00:49:55.400
in "Lost Years" is like

00:49:55.400 --> 00:49:56.434
we were basically becoming

00:49:56.434 --> 00:50:00.248
very, very serious about our faith.

00:50:00.248 --> 00:50:03.458
We had started the band.

00:50:03.458 --> 00:50:06.428
And so this was beginning
to kind of consume us

00:50:06.428 --> 00:50:09.962
and it was the way we talked--

00:50:09.962 --> 00:50:11.548
Consume our time.

00:50:11.548 --> 00:50:12.473
It didn't eat us.

00:50:12.473 --> 00:50:13.405
No, no, I--

00:50:13.405 --> 00:50:14.330
Consumed our time.

00:50:14.330 --> 00:50:15.163
Yeah, yeah.

00:50:15.163 --> 00:50:18.620
But I'm saying that our lives began--

00:50:18.620 --> 00:50:19.453
Our passions.

00:50:19.453 --> 00:50:23.870
Much more oriented towards God.

00:50:23.870 --> 00:50:24.992
Especially with the band.

00:50:24.992 --> 00:50:25.890
Yeah.

00:50:25.890 --> 00:50:27.638
We spent as much time as we could

00:50:27.638 --> 00:50:29.570
as a band.

00:50:29.570 --> 00:50:31.972
You started neglecting your basketball.

00:50:31.972 --> 00:50:34.834
I mean we were talking
more about filmmakers too.

00:50:34.834 --> 00:50:37.970
So it was like those two passions were,

00:50:37.970 --> 00:50:40.866
and then whatever girlfriend you had

00:50:40.866 --> 00:50:45.613
pretty much filled you to capacity.

00:50:45.613 --> 00:50:48.828
I'm not gonna take the bait on that one.

00:50:50.067 --> 00:50:52.070
But the,

00:50:52.070 --> 00:50:54.850
the interesting thing that
was happening with Ben

00:50:54.850 --> 00:50:57.040
is that he'd always been this,

00:50:57.040 --> 00:50:58.230
again, he's the one

00:50:58.230 --> 00:50:59.868
that had the knowledge about things, right

00:50:59.868 --> 00:51:02.676
and as he, he had all
this time on his hands

00:51:02.676 --> 00:51:05.480
where he was basically
not able to kinda get up

00:51:05.480 --> 00:51:06.470
and do things physically.

00:51:06.470 --> 00:51:07.840
The dude was reading,

00:51:07.840 --> 00:51:10.045
and then by this point, he was
on the internet all the time.

00:51:10.045 --> 00:51:12.800
And he would occasionally

00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:15.040
just kinda mention something

00:51:15.040 --> 00:51:16.603
that I would be like, wow,

00:51:16.603 --> 00:51:19.680
Ben is in a different place,

00:51:19.680 --> 00:51:21.273
spiritually.
Spiritually.

00:51:21.273 --> 00:51:23.760
He didn't go to church with us.

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:26.067
He's got lots of thoughts
about this stuff,

00:51:26.067 --> 00:51:30.122
thoughts that were to me, sort of like

00:51:30.122 --> 00:51:32.930
where is he getting this information

00:51:32.930 --> 00:51:34.140
because this isn't what,

00:51:34.140 --> 00:51:35.720
this isn't what we're
talking about in youth group.

00:51:35.720 --> 00:51:39.570
This isn't, this isn't orthodox,

00:51:39.570 --> 00:51:41.710
you know what I'm saying.

00:51:41.710 --> 00:51:42.559
Yeah.

00:51:42.559 --> 00:51:44.820
And so we always had this
kinda sense that like,

00:51:44.820 --> 00:51:47.320
you know, Ben is kinda just,

00:51:47.320 --> 00:51:48.733
he's kinda his own guy.

00:51:49.940 --> 00:51:50.863
You know, I definitely don't think

00:51:50.863 --> 00:51:53.276
he'd call himself a Christian.

00:51:53.276 --> 00:51:55.441
And that was becoming more
and more important to us.

00:51:55.441 --> 00:51:58.970
So then there was another
sort of a new roadblock--

00:51:58.970 --> 00:52:00.645
Source of tension in our friendship, yeah.

00:52:00.645 --> 00:52:02.443
This, well,

00:52:02.443 --> 00:52:04.410
am I gonna talk to him about this stuff.

00:52:04.410 --> 00:52:08.510
Because we were developing this conviction

00:52:08.510 --> 00:52:10.462
that we needed to share the gospel

00:52:10.462 --> 00:52:11.710
with our friends.

00:52:11.710 --> 00:52:14.492
I mean this is why we started the band,

00:52:14.492 --> 00:52:17.910
and we did invitations at our concerts.

00:52:17.910 --> 00:52:20.300
Like we would invite our friends out

00:52:20.300 --> 00:52:21.740
to watch us play, and then at the end,

00:52:21.740 --> 00:52:23.960
we would, we'd talk about
this in the "Lost Years" book,

00:52:23.960 --> 00:52:25.000
we would go into this thing

00:52:25.000 --> 00:52:26.830
where it's like all right
everybody, bow your heads,

00:52:26.830 --> 00:52:28.150
we're gonna pray, give you the opportunity

00:52:28.150 --> 00:52:29.390
to accept Christ.

00:52:29.390 --> 00:52:31.695
That's what we were
doing with our friends.

00:52:31.695 --> 00:52:35.500
And so, Ben's sort of ideas

00:52:35.500 --> 00:52:38.788
that were, that seemed
different and challenging

00:52:38.788 --> 00:52:43.788
began to be, I would say
even intimidating to me.

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:44.633
Hmm.

00:52:44.633 --> 00:52:47.131
Fast forward a little bit more,

00:52:47.131 --> 00:52:49.590
we go to college.

00:52:49.590 --> 00:52:52.407
We go head first into Campus Crusade.

00:52:52.407 --> 00:52:53.980
We've told that story.

00:52:53.980 --> 00:52:54.980
So if you,
Yeah.

00:52:54.980 --> 00:52:56.723
If you superimposed that on,

00:52:57.990 --> 00:53:00.683
you know, well I got an email address.

00:53:02.340 --> 00:53:05.370
I would email Ben occasionally.

00:53:05.370 --> 00:53:06.203
Yeah, at that point,

00:53:06.203 --> 00:53:07.899
it kinda resorted to email.

00:53:07.899 --> 00:53:09.590
We were off at school.

00:53:09.590 --> 00:53:10.905
He was still back at home.

00:53:10.905 --> 00:53:15.905
I think he took classes, like
college classes from home

00:53:16.620 --> 00:53:17.630
if I remember correctly.

00:53:17.630 --> 00:53:18.463
Yeah.

00:53:18.463 --> 00:53:20.300
But so, you know,

00:53:20.300 --> 00:53:21.540
there would be a couple of emails

00:53:21.540 --> 00:53:22.937
back and forth every month or two.

00:53:22.937 --> 00:53:27.317
And I remember, and it
wouldn't be the three of us.

00:53:27.317 --> 00:53:29.060
I would talk to him.

00:53:29.060 --> 00:53:30.480
You would talk to him separately, right.

00:53:30.480 --> 00:53:31.429
We didn't understand CC.

00:53:31.429 --> 00:53:33.060
Right.

00:53:33.060 --> 00:53:34.001
We didn't understand threads.

00:53:34.001 --> 00:53:37.820
I remember him saying one time,

00:53:37.820 --> 00:53:39.900
he's like I like getting emails from you

00:53:39.900 --> 00:53:41.990
because you write like you talk.

00:53:41.990 --> 00:53:43.920
That made me feel really good.

00:53:43.920 --> 00:53:45.368
I don't know what it was, but

00:53:45.368 --> 00:53:47.921
for some reason I remember
that specifically.

00:53:47.921 --> 00:53:51.326
But it wasn't, it was,

00:53:51.326 --> 00:53:55.180
the emails were far enough in between

00:53:55.180 --> 00:53:59.560
that every time we would
restart an email exchange,

00:53:59.560 --> 00:54:01.566
it would kinda be catch up.

00:54:01.566 --> 00:54:05.233
So it wasn't like we were
having an active relationship.

00:54:05.233 --> 00:54:07.550
It was more like let's,

00:54:07.550 --> 00:54:08.990
let's make sure we know what's going on

00:54:08.990 --> 00:54:10.740
in each other's lives a little bit.

00:54:11.640 --> 00:54:13.537
But again, the more you
share about your life,

00:54:13.537 --> 00:54:16.123
the more you feel like
you're sharing things

00:54:16.123 --> 00:54:18.250
that he might and to be experiencing,

00:54:18.250 --> 00:54:19.083
or isn't able to experience--

00:54:19.083 --> 00:54:20.488
Well not just that,

00:54:20.488 --> 00:54:21.321
it's like--

00:54:21.321 --> 00:54:22.351
It's hard.

00:54:22.351 --> 00:54:25.660
Our lives were more
and more about ministry

00:54:25.660 --> 00:54:28.256
and growing in our
relationship with Christ.

00:54:28.256 --> 00:54:30.030
It was like that was what it was about.

00:54:30.030 --> 00:54:32.962
And I remember we would
have these email exchanges,

00:54:32.962 --> 00:54:37.300
and I would, you know,
I'd say something about

00:54:37.300 --> 00:54:38.250
what I might be doing.

00:54:38.250 --> 00:54:40.300
And he kinda gave, he gives these,

00:54:40.300 --> 00:54:41.700
he had these perspectives,

00:54:41.700 --> 00:54:44.760
and these like interesting
thoughts about things,

00:54:44.760 --> 00:54:49.454
but I just, I couldn't engage with him.

00:54:49.454 --> 00:54:52.565
And again, there was just this,

00:54:52.565 --> 00:54:57.235
the time between correspondence
continued to increase.

00:54:57.235 --> 00:54:59.926
Then I went to,

00:54:59.926 --> 00:55:03.370
in terms of the way that I
kinda interacted with Ben

00:55:03.370 --> 00:55:05.091
while I was in college,

00:55:05.091 --> 00:55:07.720
98 I went on summer
project with Campus Crusade

00:55:07.720 --> 00:55:09.196
in New York City, as I talked about.

00:55:09.196 --> 00:55:12.014
And of course, that time,

00:55:12.014 --> 00:55:13.553
when you're that age,

00:55:13.553 --> 00:55:16.720
and you sort of put
yourself in the situation

00:55:16.720 --> 00:55:18.290
where you've been doing
nothing but ministry

00:55:18.290 --> 00:55:19.840
for two months,

00:55:19.840 --> 00:55:22.535
and it's been all about
sharing the gospel with people,

00:55:22.535 --> 00:55:26.057
and you've learned how
to do that very well.

00:55:26.057 --> 00:55:27.819
And on Campus Crusade,

00:55:27.819 --> 00:55:30.311
in Campus Crusade, that means

00:55:30.311 --> 00:55:34.270
taking people through a
presentation of the gospel,

00:55:34.270 --> 00:55:36.623
which we called the four spiritual laws,

00:55:36.623 --> 00:55:38.430
and I think now they call it

00:55:38.430 --> 00:55:40.170
the would you like to know
God personally booklet,

00:55:40.170 --> 00:55:41.730
and it's probably something different now.

00:55:41.730 --> 00:55:44.870
But essentially, it's a
short, easy to digest booklet

00:55:44.870 --> 00:55:48.590
that sort of explains
the truth of the gospel

00:55:48.590 --> 00:55:52.310
in a digestible way that
people can then make a decision

00:55:52.310 --> 00:55:53.330
on the spot.

00:55:53.330 --> 00:55:54.710
You remember what they are?

00:55:54.710 --> 00:55:55.959
God has a wonderful plan for your life.

00:55:55.959 --> 00:55:57.340
Two.

00:55:57.340 --> 00:55:58.890
We all have sinned.

00:55:58.890 --> 00:55:59.952
We are sinful, and therefore
separated from God.

00:55:59.952 --> 00:56:04.683
Three, Christ died and paid the penalty

00:56:04.683 --> 00:56:06.990
for our sins so that we could reestablish

00:56:06.990 --> 00:56:09.467
a relationship with God if point four,

00:56:09.467 --> 00:56:14.118
you make a decision to place your faith

00:56:14.118 --> 00:56:17.024
in what Christ did on your behalf.

00:56:17.024 --> 00:56:17.880
Yeah, so there you go.

00:56:17.880 --> 00:56:19.830
Maybe I just saved some people.

00:56:19.830 --> 00:56:21.450
So, I--

00:56:21.450 --> 00:56:22.283
But it was that,

00:56:22.283 --> 00:56:23.270
it was that concise.

00:56:23.270 --> 00:56:24.103
Yeah.
Right?

00:56:24.103 --> 00:56:25.393
And so it made it very sharable,

00:56:25.393 --> 00:56:28.920
which was again, the
evangelistic part of--

00:56:28.920 --> 00:56:30.520
Yeah.

00:56:30.520 --> 00:56:31.930
And so one of the things I was thinking

00:56:31.930 --> 00:56:33.100
when I got back from New York

00:56:33.100 --> 00:56:35.520
is I was like Ben is
really on my heart, right.

00:56:35.520 --> 00:56:37.562
I was like, this guy, I love this guy.

00:56:37.562 --> 00:56:39.934
And he's not a Christian.

00:56:39.934 --> 00:56:44.088
And I've got to share the gospel with him.

00:56:44.088 --> 00:56:45.940
I got to.

00:56:45.940 --> 00:56:47.653
It is my, it is my,

00:56:47.653 --> 00:56:50.480
I am mandated to share
the gospel with this guy.

00:56:50.480 --> 00:56:51.880
And I was like I agree.

00:56:51.880 --> 00:56:53.720
He was, you know,

00:56:53.720 --> 00:56:56.202
we were both real good
friends with him, but,

00:56:56.202 --> 00:57:00.070
you know, you were even
closer a friend that I was.

00:57:00.070 --> 00:57:01.167
So this is on you.

00:57:01.167 --> 00:57:03.302
Cause we both don't need to do it.

00:57:03.302 --> 00:57:05.657
Now, okay, I'm gonna tell--

00:57:05.657 --> 00:57:07.321
'Cause it seems like it
would be really tough.

00:57:07.321 --> 00:57:09.342
I'm gonna tell you what I did,

00:57:09.342 --> 00:57:11.620
and I'm gonna kinda give
you my perspective on it

00:57:13.530 --> 00:57:14.780
later.

00:57:14.780 --> 00:57:15.970
So went over there

00:57:16.890 --> 00:57:19.940
and had one of our sort of
like awkward conversations,

00:57:19.940 --> 00:57:21.190
and kinda caught up with him

00:57:21.190 --> 00:57:22.240
in what he had been doing,

00:57:22.240 --> 00:57:24.050
kinda told him about my summer.

00:57:24.050 --> 00:57:25.600
And just said hey, there's something

00:57:25.600 --> 00:57:27.320
that I'd like to share with you

00:57:28.170 --> 00:57:29.820
that I think is really important.

00:57:30.810 --> 00:57:32.060
And then I basically

00:57:33.540 --> 00:57:35.448
break out the booklet,

00:57:35.448 --> 00:57:38.203
and walk him through it.

00:57:39.045 --> 00:57:43.460
And then, I think he just
said something at the end

00:57:43.460 --> 00:57:45.390
like the whole point is that
you're supposed to bring

00:57:45.390 --> 00:57:46.915
people to point of decision.

00:57:46.915 --> 00:57:49.598
You wanna close the sale, essentially.

00:57:49.598 --> 00:57:52.280
You know, you want
people to say yes or no,

00:57:52.280 --> 00:57:53.370
and if they say yes,

00:57:53.370 --> 00:57:55.020
you wanna be, okay, would you like to pray

00:57:55.020 --> 00:57:56.261
to receive Christ right now.

00:57:56.261 --> 00:58:00.657
Ben was a little too
smart to get to that point

00:58:00.657 --> 00:58:03.886
to say, he wasn't gonna,

00:58:03.886 --> 00:58:06.135
he wasn't gonna let me get to that point.

00:58:06.135 --> 00:58:09.207
Right, meaning he was smart enough to know

00:58:09.207 --> 00:58:11.697
what you were trying to do.

00:58:11.697 --> 00:58:12.530
He saw what was happening.

00:58:12.530 --> 00:58:14.540
Because he knew that he didn't
want to make that decision,

00:58:14.540 --> 00:58:15.910
he was smart enough to like,

00:58:15.910 --> 00:58:18.050
to circumvent the awkward ending.

00:58:18.050 --> 00:58:20.077
To tactfully and kindly

00:58:20.077 --> 00:58:22.575
say I'll think about it, basically.

00:58:22.575 --> 00:58:27.575
Now, I find it very ironic that

00:58:30.630 --> 00:58:31.720
you got this guy

00:58:31.720 --> 00:58:35.260
who is, was so, so more knowledgeable

00:58:35.260 --> 00:58:37.153
about so many things than I was,

00:58:38.326 --> 00:58:42.152
who, we'd had this rich experience,

00:58:42.152 --> 00:58:44.580
and he had taught me so many things,

00:58:44.580 --> 00:58:47.740
and I had done all these
adventurous things with him

00:58:47.740 --> 00:58:49.560
because he had gone and
he had done the research,

00:58:49.560 --> 00:58:51.420
and he had read all this stuff.

00:58:51.420 --> 00:58:54.210
And then I go away,

00:58:54.210 --> 00:58:56.593
and I come back with a booklet.

00:58:57.430 --> 00:58:59.000
A pamphlet really.

00:58:59.000 --> 00:59:04.000
I come back with a one
size fits all pamphlet.

00:59:04.636 --> 00:59:08.021
And instead of having a real conversation,

00:59:08.021 --> 00:59:10.901
I'm like you wanna go through
this booklet together.

00:59:10.901 --> 00:59:12.120
Yeah.

00:59:12.120 --> 00:59:13.855
To me, it just,

00:59:13.855 --> 00:59:16.963
I cringe thinking about it now,

00:59:16.963 --> 00:59:21.870
not because I was ashamed
of the gospel at the time.

00:59:21.870 --> 00:59:22.703
I wasn't.

00:59:22.703 --> 00:59:26.923
I thought that it was
everybody needed to hear it.

00:59:28.720 --> 00:59:33.157
But the impersonal, unloving nature,

00:59:34.000 --> 00:59:35.840
I would say, and the way that I did it

00:59:35.840 --> 00:59:39.217
that didn't take into
account his circumstance.

00:59:39.217 --> 00:59:40.594
I think it--
A conversation,

00:59:40.594 --> 00:59:42.260
a real conversation--

00:59:42.260 --> 00:59:45.357
I think it was very
loving, and very cringy.

00:59:45.357 --> 00:59:46.880
I don't think it was unloving.

00:59:46.880 --> 00:59:49.320
I think it was the best that you could do

00:59:49.320 --> 00:59:52.897
based on the convictions that you held.

00:59:52.897 --> 00:59:53.730
Okay.

00:59:53.730 --> 00:59:55.660
And I don't think,

00:59:55.660 --> 00:59:57.333
and I think that Ben knew that,

00:59:58.235 --> 01:00:03.097
so and he knew that he did not
want to make that decision.

01:00:05.610 --> 01:00:09.279
So he graciously let you off the hook.

01:00:09.279 --> 01:00:11.023
You know.

01:00:12.550 --> 01:00:13.800
But I just felt--
Because I felt,

01:00:13.800 --> 01:00:14.752
didn't you--

01:00:14.752 --> 01:00:15.585
I felt like a hypocrite.

01:00:15.585 --> 01:00:17.070
Because it's like, hey, man, I went off,

01:00:17.070 --> 01:00:18.593
I lived a life.

01:00:18.593 --> 01:00:20.300
I did all this cool stuff.

01:00:20.300 --> 01:00:23.960
I kind of saw you when my mom
told told me to come see you.

01:00:23.960 --> 01:00:25.950
And then I go off and I
come back and I'm like,

01:00:25.950 --> 01:00:26.920
hey, and I've got this,

01:00:26.920 --> 01:00:28.180
I've got the good news.

01:00:28.180 --> 01:00:29.140
I've gone off.

01:00:29.140 --> 01:00:30.910
I figured this stuff out.

01:00:30.910 --> 01:00:35.620
I know everything that there
is to know about the secret to,

01:00:35.620 --> 01:00:37.318
to the, to the good life, basically.

01:00:37.318 --> 01:00:39.044
And it's all in this book.

01:00:39.044 --> 01:00:40.180
Here it is.

01:00:40.180 --> 01:00:41.150
Let's go through it together.

01:00:41.150 --> 01:00:43.116
And it just I to me,

01:00:43.116 --> 01:00:48.116
I'm offended on behalf of
Ben when I think about it,

01:00:48.586 --> 01:00:50.932
but he was gracious and he took it

01:00:50.932 --> 01:00:53.210
like a friend in a loving way.

01:00:53.210 --> 01:00:54.043
Yeah.

01:00:54.043 --> 01:00:56.210
And at least, you at the time,

01:00:56.210 --> 01:01:01.210
I feel like at least you could
share that much, you know.

01:01:01.600 --> 01:01:03.510
The ball was in his court

01:01:03.510 --> 01:01:05.440
as if maybe he didn't know those things

01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:06.500
before you shared them,

01:01:06.500 --> 01:01:08.208
I think is how I felt about it.

01:01:08.208 --> 01:01:11.285
You know, all you can do is share,

01:01:11.285 --> 01:01:13.147
and then you leave the results to God.

01:01:13.147 --> 01:01:15.383
But you feel, you feel this,

01:01:15.383 --> 01:01:19.050
you know, this tremendous pressure to,

01:01:19.050 --> 01:01:20.530
to share until you have,

01:01:20.530 --> 01:01:22.940
and then there's at least
relief like, hey, I've,

01:01:22.940 --> 01:01:24.933
I've done my part, you know.

01:01:26.860 --> 01:01:28.990
But then in retrospect,

01:01:28.990 --> 01:01:30.080
you look at all the context

01:01:30.080 --> 01:01:31.080
and you start to have the feelings

01:01:31.080 --> 01:01:33.045
that you just shared, and I,

01:01:33.045 --> 01:01:35.110
I totally get that.

01:01:35.110 --> 01:01:38.870
I mean, I didn't have that
conversation at that point.

01:01:38.870 --> 01:01:39.723
We both had,

01:01:41.040 --> 01:01:44.443
you know, other things that
we'll talk about very soon,

01:01:46.377 --> 01:01:48.960
but from there it was,

01:01:48.960 --> 01:01:50.940
I mean, when we graduated, we got married.

01:01:50.940 --> 01:01:52.498
I don't,

01:01:52.498 --> 01:01:53.331
I don't I don't think Ben

01:01:53.331 --> 01:01:55.480
was able to come to my wedding.

01:01:55.480 --> 01:01:57.434
Did Ben come to your wedding?

01:01:57.434 --> 01:02:00.500
No, I don't think so.

01:02:00.500 --> 01:02:03.530
Yeah, I don't think so either.

01:02:03.530 --> 01:02:06.944
But I think there was
still the occasional,

01:02:06.944 --> 01:02:11.456
you know, like maybe once
a year, we would see him.

01:02:11.456 --> 01:02:13.750
And it would be like,

01:02:13.750 --> 01:02:16.833
I feel so bad that it's been
so long since I've seen you.

01:02:16.833 --> 01:02:19.030
You know, you're still,

01:02:19.030 --> 01:02:20.950
you have this condition in your home,

01:02:20.950 --> 01:02:23.760
and I've moved on with my life.

01:02:23.760 --> 01:02:24.593
Now I'm married.

01:02:24.593 --> 01:02:25.786
Now I'm having kids.

01:02:25.786 --> 01:02:27.770
Now I'm, I'm,

01:02:27.770 --> 01:02:30.940
I'm leaving my engineering job

01:02:30.940 --> 01:02:32.890
and I'm joining the
staff of Campus Crusade.

01:02:32.890 --> 01:02:33.977
Now I'm leaving Campus Crusade.

01:02:33.977 --> 01:02:37.970
I think there'll be these few
and far between touch points

01:02:37.970 --> 01:02:40.690
is like, this is how my life
continues to move forward.

01:02:40.690 --> 01:02:42.400
And there will be occasional emails

01:02:42.400 --> 01:02:43.840
and based on the
conversation that we had had,

01:02:43.840 --> 01:02:45.513
we had sort of established

01:02:45.513 --> 01:02:49.910
sort of a dialogue about
philosophical, spiritual things.

01:02:49.910 --> 01:02:51.359
So occasionally, he would send something

01:02:51.359 --> 01:02:55.110
that was, you know,

01:02:55.110 --> 01:02:57.080
he was he was doing all kinds of research

01:02:57.080 --> 01:02:58.180
about all kinds of things,

01:02:58.180 --> 01:03:00.596
and he was, he wasn't
closed off by any means.

01:03:00.596 --> 01:03:03.680
He would reach out to let me know

01:03:03.680 --> 01:03:05.320
that he had read something,

01:03:05.320 --> 01:03:07.166
or he had this thought
about Jesus or whatever.

01:03:07.166 --> 01:03:09.455
Yeah, but I definitely had this,

01:03:09.455 --> 01:03:12.844
this guilt that I think is well founded

01:03:12.844 --> 01:03:16.079
that like someone who
was so important to me

01:03:16.079 --> 01:03:19.351
that I just didn't make time for.

01:03:19.351 --> 01:03:22.978
And if I did make time, it was like,

01:03:22.978 --> 01:03:25.410
it was there was still this weight

01:03:25.410 --> 01:03:29.486
of like this one issue
loomed large in my mind,

01:03:29.486 --> 01:03:33.169
and was kind of like the,

01:03:33.169 --> 01:03:36.244
maybe the real reason I've,

01:03:36.244 --> 01:03:39.722
I ultimately would reconnect over time

01:03:39.722 --> 01:03:44.722
was because this looming issue
of like, eternal destiny.

01:03:46.080 --> 01:03:47.353
Yeah, he's not saved.

01:03:47.353 --> 01:03:51.020
And then, you know, we
eventually leave staff,

01:03:51.020 --> 01:03:52.770
and we start we start our careers.

01:03:52.770 --> 01:03:57.682
I mean, we started working in
the basement in Lillington.

01:03:57.682 --> 01:04:00.540
And and making our videos from there,

01:04:00.540 --> 01:04:02.700
as we, as we got going.

01:04:02.700 --> 01:04:04.897
Some of the exchanges
that we'd have with him,

01:04:04.897 --> 01:04:06.935
again, by that point,
we were in Lillington,

01:04:06.935 --> 01:04:10.828
we were 10 minutes away from his house.

01:04:10.828 --> 01:04:11.855
Yeah.

01:04:11.855 --> 01:04:14.130
And then it was like,
yeah, we should see Ben.

01:04:14.130 --> 01:04:17.260
We should go by and see
him, but we got to work.

01:04:17.260 --> 01:04:18.093
We got to work.

01:04:18.093 --> 01:04:18.926
And then I gotta go,

01:04:18.926 --> 01:04:20.144
I gotta drive the opposite
direction 15 minutes

01:04:20.144 --> 01:04:24.203
in order to like, help with
the baby and like, you know,

01:04:24.203 --> 01:04:26.600
continue to do my life

01:04:26.600 --> 01:04:28.186
because there's so much
going on in my life.

01:04:28.186 --> 01:04:32.630
But I know that he knew
about the work we were doing.

01:04:32.630 --> 01:04:34.860
He got a big kick out of
the songs that we'd write

01:04:34.860 --> 01:04:37.400
and like the music videos
that we would make,

01:04:37.400 --> 01:04:39.584
and so like there'll be
some email exchanges there.

01:04:39.584 --> 01:04:43.983
Do you remember when we got the news?

01:04:46.350 --> 01:04:47.540
Like how we heard?

01:04:47.540 --> 01:04:48.840
I don't.

01:04:48.840 --> 01:04:51.080
I don't remember specifically either.

01:04:51.080 --> 01:04:54.070
But we,

01:04:54.070 --> 01:04:55.551
I, yes,

01:04:55.551 --> 01:04:57.050
I don't remember how we heard,

01:04:57.050 --> 01:05:00.790
but we heard from someone.

01:05:00.790 --> 01:05:02.480
And it was kind of going around the county

01:05:02.480 --> 01:05:03.930
that like Ben was sick.

01:05:03.930 --> 01:05:06.410
It's like, well, Ben's
kinda always been sick.

01:05:06.410 --> 01:05:08.590
Now Ben is really sick.

01:05:08.590 --> 01:05:09.993
Ben's got cancer.

01:05:10.830 --> 01:05:12.640
Oh man.

01:05:12.640 --> 01:05:15.150
Ben has cancer.

01:05:15.150 --> 01:05:16.080
Is it connected?

01:05:16.080 --> 01:05:17.599
Well, people didn't know.

01:05:17.599 --> 01:05:19.550
You know, and then--

01:05:19.550 --> 01:05:20.630
Did we go see him?

01:05:20.630 --> 01:05:23.000
I have to think we went
and saw him when we,

01:05:23.000 --> 01:05:27.421
when we got that news that
he had cancer at his house.

01:05:27.421 --> 01:05:30.525
I definitely think as
he started to get worse,

01:05:30.525 --> 01:05:33.020
it was, it was testicular cancer--

01:05:33.020 --> 01:05:34.069
That moved very quickly.

01:05:34.069 --> 01:05:35.380
That moved very quickly,

01:05:35.380 --> 01:05:37.576
by the time they caught it,

01:05:37.576 --> 01:05:42.576
it had, it had spread
and it was really bad.

01:05:42.689 --> 01:05:44.913
And then we, we,

01:05:44.913 --> 01:05:48.518
once we found out we saw him
a few times at his house,

01:05:48.518 --> 01:05:53.518
and every time he was
looking worse and worse.

01:05:57.517 --> 01:06:00.470
And then we got the the word.

01:06:01.490 --> 01:06:04.447
I mean do your remember
visiting him at his house?

01:06:04.447 --> 01:06:05.460
I,

01:06:05.460 --> 01:06:06.320
honestly

01:06:08.690 --> 01:06:11.280
I, not until after we had visit him,

01:06:11.280 --> 01:06:13.370
visited him at hospice.

01:06:13.370 --> 01:06:15.470
They put him in hospice,

01:06:15.470 --> 01:06:19.387
which was basically an end
of life care, you're dying.

01:06:19.387 --> 01:06:21.770
So we're going to make you
as comfortable as possible.

01:06:21.770 --> 01:06:23.360
And we're going to give
you around the clock care

01:06:23.360 --> 01:06:26.429
in a facility, which
now was in Lillington.

01:06:26.429 --> 01:06:27.980
Oh, and it was it was--

01:06:27.980 --> 01:06:29.390
It was on our way from--

01:06:29.390 --> 01:06:30.620
A mile from our--

01:06:30.620 --> 01:06:32.150
We would carpool from Fu Quay--

01:06:32.150 --> 01:06:33.050
Office.

01:06:33.050 --> 01:06:34.670
And we'd go past this facility,

01:06:34.670 --> 01:06:35.880
hang a right,

01:06:35.880 --> 01:06:38.913
and then go to our studio.

01:06:38.913 --> 01:06:42.247
So you know, the plan was,

01:06:42.247 --> 01:06:45.191
well at least once a week,

01:06:45.191 --> 01:06:47.086
we'll go by and see him,

01:06:47.086 --> 01:06:49.233
I think is what we,

01:06:50.320 --> 01:06:51.650
you know, it's like we were scrambling.

01:06:51.650 --> 01:06:53.780
We were trying to make, make our,

01:06:53.780 --> 01:06:55.700
make our lives work.

01:06:55.700 --> 01:06:56.790
But on our way into work,

01:06:56.790 --> 01:06:59.323
we can go by there and
see him for an hour.

01:07:02.130 --> 01:07:03.490
So it's, I mean, it's,

01:07:03.490 --> 01:07:05.138
if you've never seen anybody in hospice,

01:07:05.138 --> 01:07:07.030
it's kind of, it's like a hospital room.

01:07:07.030 --> 01:07:08.630
There's a hospital bed in there,

01:07:13.523 --> 01:07:14.963
and I don't know how long he was there.

01:07:14.963 --> 01:07:18.235
I, my recollection is
that it was a few months.

01:07:18.235 --> 01:07:19.760
I don't think so.

01:07:19.760 --> 01:07:20.963
Maybe it was six weeks.

01:07:20.963 --> 01:07:22.870
You think it might have been four weeks?

01:07:22.870 --> 01:07:26.063
I, in my recollection, he
was there for a few weeks.

01:07:26.063 --> 01:07:29.860
And then they were like, well,

01:07:29.860 --> 01:07:31.780
he's gonna, he's gonna pass away,

01:07:31.780 --> 01:07:34.010
and so we want that to
happen at his house.

01:07:34.010 --> 01:07:36.910
And so they basically put the hospital bed

01:07:36.910 --> 01:07:39.773
and everything in the house, downstairs.

01:07:43.160 --> 01:07:44.908
But, so to kind of get,

01:07:44.908 --> 01:07:48.410
you know, there was this,

01:07:48.410 --> 01:07:49.750
there was a lot of guilt

01:07:52.080 --> 01:07:53.420
associated with

01:07:55.710 --> 01:07:56.560
you know, having,

01:08:10.070 --> 01:08:11.373
having not seen him,

01:08:20.530 --> 01:08:23.230
and but the main thing that
we were thinking about was

01:08:24.870 --> 01:08:26.430
this guy is going to die.

01:08:26.430 --> 01:08:27.360
Yeah.

01:08:27.360 --> 01:08:29.393
And we got, and we had to save him.

01:08:30.440 --> 01:08:31.350
Yeah, we saw him.

01:08:31.350 --> 01:08:32.660
I mean we would visit him.

01:08:32.660 --> 01:08:34.090
We visited him a few times.

01:08:34.090 --> 01:08:34.923
And it was,

01:08:36.360 --> 01:08:39.743
it was, he was doing bad but it was,

01:08:41.100 --> 01:08:44.040
he was, he always had a smile for us.

01:08:44.040 --> 01:08:46.460
He had the same sense of
humor that he always had.

01:08:46.460 --> 01:08:47.293
He had--

01:08:47.293 --> 01:08:48.257
He'd crack a joke,

01:08:48.257 --> 01:08:49.800
something about his balls

01:08:49.800 --> 01:08:51.577
because that was where the cancer started.

01:08:51.577 --> 01:08:53.770
Yeah, I remember him saying

01:08:57.080 --> 01:08:59.010
you know, he, he said

01:09:00.360 --> 01:09:01.608
do you want to see it?

01:09:01.608 --> 01:09:02.441
Yeah.

01:09:02.441 --> 01:09:04.380
And then he, you know,

01:09:04.380 --> 01:09:05.850
he just had a hospital gown on

01:09:05.850 --> 01:09:07.243
and we like looked at each other.

01:09:07.243 --> 01:09:09.975
It was just the three of us in that room.

01:09:09.975 --> 01:09:14.085
And we're like, I guess.

01:09:14.085 --> 01:09:16.110
And he can't, you know, he had this,

01:09:16.110 --> 01:09:18.210
this smile on his face like,

01:09:18.210 --> 01:09:21.410
this is, this is funny.

01:09:21.410 --> 01:09:24.918
And then he like he showed
us his testicle that was,

01:09:24.918 --> 01:09:27.017
like, as big as an orange.

01:09:27.017 --> 01:09:28.320
Yeah.

01:09:28.320 --> 01:09:30.030
I mean, like,

01:09:30.030 --> 01:09:32.620
and then he made a joke about it.

01:09:32.620 --> 01:09:36.749
And we laughed, because that's who he was.

01:09:36.749 --> 01:09:37.675
Yeah.

01:09:37.675 --> 01:09:39.733
And he, he had the ability to do that.

01:09:41.440 --> 01:09:42.573
And then he said,

01:09:43.990 --> 01:09:47.520
if you, if you think something's wrong,

01:09:47.520 --> 01:09:50.140
don't ignore it, because
that's what I did.

01:09:50.140 --> 01:09:50.973
Yeah.

01:09:55.620 --> 01:09:58.120
And I think,

01:09:58.120 --> 01:10:02.270
I think that may have
been in our last visit.

01:10:02.270 --> 01:10:06.300
Well, what I remember is
that we would go see him.

01:10:06.300 --> 01:10:09.837
And then every time we saw him,

01:10:09.837 --> 01:10:13.670
we would turn the conversation
towards spiritual things.

01:10:13.670 --> 01:10:15.837
So regardless of how it started,

01:10:15.837 --> 01:10:18.090
we like Ben, you know,

01:10:18.090 --> 01:10:21.030
I know we haven't talked
about it that much.

01:10:21.030 --> 01:10:23.410
I know I talked about it
talk to you about this

01:10:23.410 --> 01:10:26.740
when I was in college, but you know,

01:10:26.740 --> 01:10:28.440
have you thought about
your eternal destiny?

01:10:28.440 --> 01:10:29.860
Have you thought about
where you're gonna go?

01:10:29.860 --> 01:10:32.720
Or we might just say it.

01:10:32.720 --> 01:10:34.130
I don't remember exactly how he said it.

01:10:34.130 --> 01:10:36.824
But I think some ways it was more like,

01:10:36.824 --> 01:10:38.183
well you know that God loves you.

01:10:38.183 --> 01:10:39.790
And, you know, we would say things

01:10:39.790 --> 01:10:42.890
that were more like
encouraging platitudes.

01:10:42.890 --> 01:10:44.390
Well, I specifically,

01:10:44.390 --> 01:10:46.160
I mean, the way I remember it,

01:10:46.160 --> 01:10:47.900
I would ask him questions like,

01:10:47.900 --> 01:10:49.420
what do you think about God now?

01:10:49.420 --> 01:10:50.380
Yeah, I remember that.

01:10:50.380 --> 01:10:51.840
Where are you at with the way

01:10:51.840 --> 01:10:53.182
that you think about Jesus?

01:10:53.182 --> 01:10:56.040
You know, this dude thought a lot

01:10:56.040 --> 01:10:57.590
about the spiritual things,

01:10:57.590 --> 01:10:59.130
and he had a spirituality.

01:10:59.130 --> 01:11:01.309
He wasn't an atheist.

01:11:01.309 --> 01:11:02.720
He was definitely,

01:11:02.720 --> 01:11:05.630
I would say he was agnostic
because he had this--

01:11:05.630 --> 01:11:06.610
He said I didn't know,

01:11:06.610 --> 01:11:07.676
I don't know.

01:11:07.676 --> 01:11:08.891
He was like I just--

01:11:08.891 --> 01:11:09.843
I just don't know.
Don't know.

01:11:09.843 --> 01:11:11.410
And I remember

01:11:11.410 --> 01:11:15.250
one time basically pressing,

01:11:15.250 --> 01:11:17.520
I was like, this is,

01:11:17.520 --> 01:11:18.980
I know this is offensive,

01:11:18.980 --> 01:11:20.660
and this is tough and the dude is dying,

01:11:20.660 --> 01:11:23.200
but this is his soul is worth it.

01:11:23.200 --> 01:11:24.033
Yeah.

01:11:24.033 --> 01:11:26.850
And and I think, in this particular time,

01:11:26.850 --> 01:11:31.093
we had the sense that it was
close to the end for him.

01:11:31.093 --> 01:11:34.801
We had been talking about
in our previous visits,

01:11:34.801 --> 01:11:37.103
his preparations.

01:11:40.522 --> 01:11:41.355
He said,

01:11:43.820 --> 01:11:45.310
I want you guys.

01:11:45.310 --> 01:11:47.480
I want you guys to sing at my funeral.

01:11:47.480 --> 01:11:48.313
Yeah.

01:11:48.313 --> 01:11:50.585
And we were like, oh, gosh.

01:11:50.585 --> 01:11:52.010
Okay.

01:11:52.010 --> 01:11:54.760
And he said, I want you to
sing one of your funny songs,

01:11:54.760 --> 01:11:56.094
like the corn hole song.

01:11:57.200 --> 01:11:59.043
Like that, again, that was his,

01:11:59.043 --> 01:12:01.060
that was his way.

01:12:01.060 --> 01:12:02.749
He was like, I think it would.

01:12:02.749 --> 01:12:04.700
I was like, well that's
gonna be weird, Ben.

01:12:04.700 --> 01:12:05.620
He's like--

01:12:05.620 --> 01:12:06.453
Exactly.

01:12:06.453 --> 01:12:07.530
Exactly.

01:12:07.530 --> 01:12:08.673
That's what he wanted.

01:12:08.673 --> 01:12:10.056
I want you to sing the corn
hole song at my funeral.

01:12:10.056 --> 01:12:11.113
And,

01:12:13.630 --> 01:12:14.473
he said,

01:12:16.615 --> 01:12:18.365
you know, we said, Ben, we want to,

01:12:19.687 --> 01:12:20.770
I don't know if he said
it or if we said it,

01:12:20.770 --> 01:12:24.593
but he wanted to go to
the river one more time.

01:12:25.650 --> 01:12:29.140
And so I remember, we
mentioned that to his parents,

01:12:29.140 --> 01:12:30.354
like, you know, he wants
to go to the river,

01:12:30.354 --> 01:12:31.760
and they were like, we don't,

01:12:31.760 --> 01:12:33.870
we don't know if he's able,

01:12:33.870 --> 01:12:35.160
like if we take a wheelchair,

01:12:35.160 --> 01:12:36.940
and like, just take him
to the edge of the river

01:12:36.940 --> 01:12:38.376
where you guys used to swim is like,

01:12:38.376 --> 01:12:41.530
if you think that we could do that,

01:12:41.530 --> 01:12:42.933
we would want to do that.

01:12:47.020 --> 01:12:50.550
The next time we visited
was after that conversation,

01:12:50.550 --> 01:12:52.435
and we got a sense that
it was the last time

01:12:52.435 --> 01:12:56.600
and that's when he showed us
his balls and made us laugh,

01:12:56.600 --> 01:12:59.576
but I remember before you went in,

01:12:59.576 --> 01:13:03.790
his older sister, who we
had known our whole lives,

01:13:03.790 --> 01:13:05.663
said to us,

01:13:06.855 --> 01:13:08.543
guys just,

01:13:09.750 --> 01:13:13.940
just try not to talk
about religious, religion.

01:13:13.940 --> 01:13:15.070
Like that's how she put it.

01:13:15.070 --> 01:13:17.060
She was like, I don't want you to--

01:13:17.060 --> 01:13:18.008
I think she--

01:13:18.008 --> 01:13:22.410
We don't wanna upset him at
this point in his process.

01:13:22.410 --> 01:13:26.001
Well, I think what was happening is,

01:13:26.001 --> 01:13:28.763
you know, he loved seeing us.

01:13:30.166 --> 01:13:35.166
But we were so singularly
focused on bringing him

01:13:38.520 --> 01:13:39.492
faith--
Helping him.

01:13:39.492 --> 01:13:41.253
That--
We felt.

01:13:43.610 --> 01:13:45.530
The conversation would get,

01:13:45.530 --> 01:13:47.380
I recall one time

01:13:47.380 --> 01:13:50.060
where I really, I just really pressed in,

01:13:50.060 --> 01:13:52.490
and I said, Ben, you know,

01:13:52.490 --> 01:13:54.220
what do you have to lose?

01:13:54.220 --> 01:13:56.260
If just just pray a prayer with us

01:13:56.260 --> 01:13:57.531
to make this decision.

01:13:57.531 --> 01:13:58.364
This was the last meeting.

01:13:58.364 --> 01:13:59.390
Yeah.

01:13:59.390 --> 01:14:00.450
But this was the last meeting

01:14:00.450 --> 01:14:01.640
before his sister told us

01:14:01.640 --> 01:14:02.710
to not to talk about it anymore

01:14:02.710 --> 01:14:04.083
'cause we honored that.

01:14:06.720 --> 01:14:09.090
And what happened was,

01:14:09.090 --> 01:14:11.728
is he said, I don't know.

01:14:11.728 --> 01:14:13.040
I don't know.

01:14:13.040 --> 01:14:14.040
I can't be sure.

01:14:14.040 --> 01:14:15.510
And he started crying.

01:14:15.510 --> 01:14:16.343
Yeah.

01:14:17.812 --> 01:14:18.760
And, you know, the thing is it the time

01:14:18.760 --> 01:14:22.922
I felt bad, but I was like, well,

01:14:22.922 --> 01:14:25.740
crying a little bit now

01:14:25.740 --> 01:14:27.670
is not anything compared
to what will happen to him

01:14:27.670 --> 01:14:28.920
if he doesn't make this decision.

01:14:28.920 --> 01:14:31.460
I mean, that's the logic that
was happening in my brain.

01:14:31.460 --> 01:14:32.293
Yeah.

01:14:32.293 --> 01:14:33.741
And we had talked about it ahead of time

01:14:33.741 --> 01:14:36.290
that we had to do,

01:14:36.290 --> 01:14:39.300
we just felt like we had
to be as clear as possible

01:14:39.300 --> 01:14:41.856
and do everything we could to help him.

01:14:41.856 --> 01:14:44.949
So this I was not blindsided
by you pressing him.

01:14:44.949 --> 01:14:47.105
We had agreed upon it.

01:14:47.105 --> 01:14:48.340
Yeah.

01:14:48.340 --> 01:14:50.920
And I,

01:14:50.920 --> 01:14:53.587
I mean, we would leave those
meetings and I would just ball

01:14:53.587 --> 01:14:55.793
like on the on the way back home.

01:14:58.670 --> 01:14:59.853
You know, feeling,

01:15:02.420 --> 01:15:04.630
feeling responsible for that?

01:15:04.630 --> 01:15:05.930
Yeah.

01:15:05.930 --> 01:15:07.307
And worrying about him.

01:15:08.170 --> 01:15:11.140
But his sister told us
the next time we went,

01:15:11.140 --> 01:15:12.477
and she said, you know--

01:15:12.477 --> 01:15:14.290
But you said you pressed him,

01:15:14.290 --> 01:15:18.140
and I mean, to pray with us, right?

01:15:18.140 --> 01:15:19.599
Yeah.

01:15:19.599 --> 01:15:21.283
So,

01:15:22.770 --> 01:15:24.162
yeah, that's what you meant by that was,

01:15:24.162 --> 01:15:27.220
well, I don't remember
exactly how we said it,

01:15:27.220 --> 01:15:28.900
but it was, you know,

01:15:28.900 --> 01:15:30.410
you don't have to know.

01:15:30.410 --> 01:15:33.630
It's just let's just pray

01:15:33.630 --> 01:15:36.850
and you can, we can pray something,

01:15:36.850 --> 01:15:38.163
you can repeat after us,

01:15:38.163 --> 01:15:41.750
and you can, you can

01:15:43.070 --> 01:15:44.433
accept Jesus.

01:15:45.600 --> 01:15:46.433
I mean,

01:15:48.700 --> 01:15:51.393
and he said, okay.

01:15:52.230 --> 01:15:54.710
And he, you know, it was basically

01:15:58.738 --> 01:16:02.070
we prayed, you know, and it was,

01:16:02.070 --> 01:16:03.970
it's one of those things that like,

01:16:03.970 --> 01:16:06.693
you can look at it at the
time afterward and say,

01:16:08.130 --> 01:16:08.970
okay, I feel,

01:16:08.970 --> 01:16:10.398
I feel relieved.

01:16:10.398 --> 01:16:13.030
Maybe that did it.

01:16:13.030 --> 01:16:15.600
I feel, I mean, the words were right.

01:16:15.600 --> 01:16:17.480
We can't control,

01:16:17.480 --> 01:16:19.370
like, what's going on in his heart.

01:16:19.370 --> 01:16:23.453
But you know, he did say the words.

01:16:24.685 --> 01:16:28.630
And it was kind of reduced to that.

01:16:28.630 --> 01:16:29.637
Like a--

01:16:29.637 --> 01:16:30.840
Yeah.

01:16:30.840 --> 01:16:32.290
From an outsider's perspective,

01:16:32.290 --> 01:16:35.250
you might think of it as
like a magical incantation.

01:16:35.250 --> 01:16:36.880
That's not how we thought of it.

01:16:36.880 --> 01:16:37.950
But it was.

01:16:37.950 --> 01:16:39.437
If he was sincere in his heart, then--

01:16:39.437 --> 01:16:41.135
Then that--
Then he was saved.

01:16:41.135 --> 01:16:43.432
And only God knows.
Yeah.

01:16:43.432 --> 01:16:46.783
So yeah--

01:16:46.783 --> 01:16:48.330
And I don't know if he, you know,

01:16:48.330 --> 01:16:49.163
if he if he did it for us,

01:16:49.163 --> 01:16:50.227
or if he did it for him.

01:16:50.227 --> 01:16:51.710
But the thing is is that--

01:16:51.710 --> 01:16:53.054
Or both probably.

01:16:53.054 --> 01:16:56.806
You know, it's just so
hard to process because

01:16:56.806 --> 01:17:01.505
it gave us this sense
of closure that okay,

01:17:01.505 --> 01:17:03.166
all right, we did everything we could.

01:17:03.166 --> 01:17:08.090
And I think that we
were acting out of love

01:17:08.090 --> 01:17:10.460
because of the way that we thought

01:17:10.460 --> 01:17:11.760
about the world at the time.

01:17:11.760 --> 01:17:12.610
He knew that.

01:17:12.610 --> 01:17:13.443
He knew that.

01:17:13.443 --> 01:17:14.913
And you know,

01:17:17.010 --> 01:17:20.233
so I don't blame us for
doing something that was,

01:17:21.352 --> 01:17:24.070
you know, I wish we could've spent,

01:17:24.070 --> 01:17:26.960
I wish we could've spent
a different kind of time.

01:17:26.960 --> 01:17:27.793
Yeah.

01:17:27.793 --> 01:17:28.820
That's opposed to like,

01:17:29.930 --> 01:17:31.680
trying to convince him of something

01:17:35.520 --> 01:17:37.317
that I don't even believe any more.

01:17:37.317 --> 01:17:38.853
You know.

01:17:40.300 --> 01:17:41.133
Yeah.

01:17:43.050 --> 01:17:43.883
So there was,

01:17:43.883 --> 01:17:45.160
if there was one more meeting at the,

01:17:45.160 --> 01:17:46.863
then that was it.

01:17:48.770 --> 01:17:49.990
I don't recall seeing him

01:17:49.990 --> 01:17:51.580
when they moved him back to his house,

01:17:51.580 --> 01:17:55.442
I again I, this may be
the nature of memory.

01:17:55.442 --> 01:17:57.342
I have a,

01:17:57.342 --> 01:18:01.343
a recollection of talking
to him once in his home.

01:18:04.345 --> 01:18:05.178
But I made,

01:18:05.178 --> 01:18:06.360
that may be something
that I've just created

01:18:06.360 --> 01:18:07.890
because I've been in
his home so many times,

01:18:07.890 --> 01:18:09.120
and they told me where the bed was,

01:18:09.120 --> 01:18:11.170
and but I seem to remember,

01:18:11.170 --> 01:18:13.097
no, I think I did talk to him because

01:18:13.097 --> 01:18:15.927
he was getting a little
delirious at the end.

01:18:15.927 --> 01:18:17.120
I wasn't there for this.

01:18:17.120 --> 01:18:20.040
And he had actually, you know,

01:18:20.040 --> 01:18:21.300
so had like a catheter,

01:18:21.300 --> 01:18:23.733
and he got up in the
middle of night and like,

01:18:23.733 --> 01:18:26.390
walked and didn't realize
it was in and stuff,

01:18:26.390 --> 01:18:27.320
and they were just like, it's,

01:18:27.320 --> 01:18:28.320
you know, he was telling me that

01:18:28.320 --> 01:18:30.420
and he was kind of talking
about it as a funny story.

01:18:30.420 --> 01:18:33.270
Like, let me tell you what
happened to me last night.

01:18:33.270 --> 01:18:36.870
But then it quickly got to a place where,

01:18:36.870 --> 01:18:38.270
you know, his mom said that,

01:18:39.320 --> 01:18:42.560
you know, you don't you don't
want to see him like this.

01:18:42.560 --> 01:18:43.393
He's dying.

01:18:44.270 --> 01:18:46.103
When we got the call it was,

01:18:47.930 --> 01:18:51.780
I mean, we knew the call was coming.

01:18:51.780 --> 01:18:53.137
So we weren't blindsided by it,

01:18:53.137 --> 01:18:55.443
and I think at the time we didn't feel,

01:18:59.230 --> 01:19:00.810
we felt like we had,

01:19:00.810 --> 01:19:02.500
we had done our piece.

01:19:02.500 --> 01:19:04.910
You know, I think that's
how we felt about it then,

01:19:04.910 --> 01:19:07.033
not how we feel about it right now.

01:19:08.400 --> 01:19:09.233
Yeah.

01:19:09.233 --> 01:19:11.433
And yeah, they,

01:19:12.440 --> 01:19:13.893
they asked us to,

01:19:16.480 --> 01:19:18.808
to, they said there'll be
an opportunity to share

01:19:18.808 --> 01:19:21.083
at the graveside service.

01:19:22.460 --> 01:19:24.390
And either they asked us to sing a song,

01:19:24.390 --> 01:19:26.595
or we volunteered and said that he--

01:19:26.595 --> 01:19:27.921
He asked us to, but--

01:19:27.921 --> 01:19:30.223
But we didn't sing the corn hole song.

01:19:30.223 --> 01:19:31.615
We knew we could not--
Spoiler alert.

01:19:31.615 --> 01:19:33.748
Sing the corn hole song.

01:19:33.748 --> 01:19:36.621
But we thought that the perfect compromise

01:19:36.621 --> 01:19:39.580
was, and of course this was,

01:19:39.580 --> 01:19:41.640
this has a whole different
meaning now, but,

01:19:41.640 --> 01:19:43.553
you know, it didn't at the time,

01:19:46.200 --> 01:19:48.480
to sing the Michael Jackson song, "Ben",

01:19:48.480 --> 01:19:51.170
which is about your pet rat.

01:19:51.170 --> 01:19:52.180
A killer rat

01:19:53.175 --> 01:19:57.538
who was Michael Jackson's pet.

01:19:57.538 --> 01:20:01.303
Yeah, and I pulled up the lyrics.

01:20:03.140 --> 01:20:04.930
Yeah, you learned it on the guitar.

01:20:04.930 --> 01:20:08.733
We sang it in harmony at
the graveside service.

01:20:11.120 --> 01:20:13.623
Ben, most people would turn you away.

01:20:14.780 --> 01:20:17.510
I don't listen to a word they say.

01:20:17.510 --> 01:20:19.533
They don't see you as I do.

01:20:21.155 --> 01:20:22.338
I wish they would try to.

01:20:22.338 --> 01:20:25.570
I'm sure they'd think again,

01:20:25.570 --> 01:20:27.433
if they had a friend like Ben.

01:20:28.860 --> 01:20:31.820
So we made a joke about it being a song

01:20:31.820 --> 01:20:34.406
about Michael Jackson and a rat.

01:20:34.406 --> 01:20:36.235
Which was very appropriate for Ben.

01:20:36.235 --> 01:20:37.920
That he would love that,

01:20:37.920 --> 01:20:39.920
and everybody agreed who was there.

01:20:39.920 --> 01:20:44.760
And it was, it was a
difficult song to sing.

01:20:44.760 --> 01:20:46.040
But--

01:20:46.040 --> 01:20:47.910
Well, and do you remember what we said?

01:20:47.910 --> 01:20:51.230
Because I remember, in
addition to the song--

01:20:51.230 --> 01:20:52.260
I remember,

01:20:52.260 --> 01:20:54.353
I think I know what
you're gonna say, yeah.

01:20:55.460 --> 01:20:56.760
Again, you know,

01:20:56.760 --> 01:20:58.363
interesting thing is that at the time,

01:20:58.363 --> 01:21:00.700
it wasn't that I wasn't, you know,

01:21:00.700 --> 01:21:02.413
as I said in the stories,

01:21:03.310 --> 01:21:05.970
it wasn't that I hadn't doubted things

01:21:05.970 --> 01:21:07.580
pretty significantly at that point,

01:21:07.580 --> 01:21:11.807
but it hadn't gotten to
the core of my faith yet.

01:21:14.830 --> 01:21:17.268
So, so, this didn't create a crisis.

01:21:17.268 --> 01:21:21.012
It created a moment where
we had to believe that Ben--

01:21:21.012 --> 01:21:22.220
He was in heaven.

01:21:22.220 --> 01:21:23.053
He was in heaven.

01:21:23.053 --> 01:21:26.166
And that's what I told
everybody at that funeral.

01:21:26.166 --> 01:21:28.110
I was like, you know--

01:21:28.110 --> 01:21:29.180
He did have faith.

01:21:29.180 --> 01:21:30.737
I didn't go into the details

01:21:30.737 --> 01:21:34.130
of what the story that happened

01:21:34.130 --> 01:21:35.650
because I thought that
might be inappropriate.

01:21:35.650 --> 01:21:36.886
But--

01:21:36.886 --> 01:21:37.809
Yeah.

01:21:37.809 --> 01:21:38.642
We basically said that, you know,

01:21:38.642 --> 01:21:40.350
again, this is one of the things that

01:21:43.120 --> 01:21:44.430
is so interesting.

01:21:44.430 --> 01:21:46.770
I've been to lots of funerals.

01:21:46.770 --> 01:21:48.480
And I haven't, I've,

01:21:48.480 --> 01:21:50.410
every one I've been to for the most part

01:21:50.410 --> 01:21:52.732
has been a Christian funeral.

01:21:52.732 --> 01:21:54.600
You know, there's a certain,

01:21:54.600 --> 01:21:57.810
it's, it's ironic, because there's a hope

01:21:57.810 --> 01:22:01.330
that comes with faith,

01:22:01.330 --> 01:22:02.717
the idea that people go on

01:22:02.717 --> 01:22:04.230
and that you're going to see people again,

01:22:04.230 --> 01:22:06.823
and you're going to see people in heaven.

01:22:08.504 --> 01:22:10.740
Which may be true.

01:22:10.740 --> 01:22:13.250
And but then the flip
side of that is that,

01:22:13.250 --> 01:22:16.892
well, if you subscribe to
a particular, you know,

01:22:16.892 --> 01:22:20.330
understanding of Christianity,

01:22:20.330 --> 01:22:22.170
well, some people are not
going to be in heaven,

01:22:22.170 --> 01:22:23.860
they're going to be in hell.

01:22:23.860 --> 01:22:25.460
And those funerals are awkward

01:22:25.460 --> 01:22:28.866
when someone is not saved,
and everybody knows it.

01:22:28.866 --> 01:22:31.010
It's like, well, he lived a good life.

01:22:31.010 --> 01:22:33.590
Let's tell some, let's tell
some stories about him.

01:22:33.590 --> 01:22:34.930
And let's just hope that God

01:22:34.930 --> 01:22:36.914
has mercy on his soul essentially.

01:22:36.914 --> 01:22:41.510
Which I just always find
so, you know, I get it.

01:22:41.510 --> 01:22:42.670
I understand it.

01:22:42.670 --> 01:22:44.132
That's just the way
you see the world then,

01:22:44.132 --> 01:22:46.780
that's the kind of the way
that you're going to respond.

01:22:46.780 --> 01:22:48.610
But for us for Ben,

01:22:48.610 --> 01:22:51.068
because we had gotten him to
the point to pray that prayer,

01:22:51.068 --> 01:22:54.768
we could have this
perspective that was like,

01:22:54.768 --> 01:22:58.150
Ben left this world with faith

01:22:58.150 --> 01:22:58.983
and that's what,

01:22:58.983 --> 01:23:00.395
that's why we're going to see him again.

01:23:00.395 --> 01:23:02.519
And we were able to say that.

01:23:02.519 --> 01:23:05.650
And I don't know, it's it's interesting,

01:23:05.650 --> 01:23:08.427
because now looking back on it,

01:23:08.427 --> 01:23:10.484
and the way that I see the world now,

01:23:10.484 --> 01:23:15.440
and I'm like, yeah, maybe
I'll see Ben again one day.

01:23:15.440 --> 01:23:16.273
I don't know.

01:23:16.273 --> 01:23:17.930
I don't think that I can know.

01:23:17.930 --> 01:23:20.943
And I no longer really
care that I can know.

01:23:22.667 --> 01:23:27.667
But I, I see what we did
was very much about us.

01:23:30.270 --> 01:23:31.970
I'm not saying we didn't love Ben.

01:23:32.840 --> 01:23:35.030
I'm not saying we didn't
actually care about him.

01:23:35.030 --> 01:23:36.700
And I'm not saying that
we weren't motivated

01:23:36.700 --> 01:23:39.120
by a concern for his soul.

01:23:39.120 --> 01:23:41.910
But I also think that a part,

01:23:41.910 --> 01:23:44.340
a not insignificant part of that process

01:23:44.340 --> 01:23:46.640
was about us feeling good,

01:23:46.640 --> 01:23:49.640
and being okay with ourselves,

01:23:49.640 --> 01:23:52.440
and being able to say that we did our job,

01:23:52.440 --> 01:23:53.680
we did our duty,

01:23:53.680 --> 01:23:55.050
and now we can get up at the funeral

01:23:55.050 --> 01:23:56.566
and say that he's got faith.

01:23:56.566 --> 01:23:59.030
And, you know--

01:23:59.030 --> 01:24:00.174
I mean, I don't--

01:24:00.174 --> 01:24:01.551
I don't believe blame us, but it was--

01:24:01.551 --> 01:24:03.133
I don't think it was the majority selfish.

01:24:03.133 --> 01:24:05.290
I think they were,

01:24:05.290 --> 01:24:06.860
I think there were elements to that.

01:24:06.860 --> 01:24:09.110
I think that we just,

01:24:09.110 --> 01:24:11.150
we needed to have that hope,

01:24:11.150 --> 01:24:12.506
because we loved him so much.

01:24:12.506 --> 01:24:13.400
Yeah.

01:24:13.400 --> 01:24:16.188
And we wanted to share it because

01:24:16.188 --> 01:24:19.160
we wanted to say something at the funeral

01:24:19.160 --> 01:24:21.160
because if there was anybody,

01:24:21.160 --> 01:24:24.590
we wanted to help anyone else

01:24:24.590 --> 01:24:26.570
that felt the same way that we felt.

01:24:26.570 --> 01:24:27.403
Yeah.

01:24:27.403 --> 01:24:31.865
But I don't know how it
made his family feel.

01:24:31.865 --> 01:24:35.210
And it probably didn't
make 'em feel better.

01:24:35.210 --> 01:24:37.924
And I also--

01:24:37.924 --> 01:24:39.318
I think they understood, right.

01:24:39.318 --> 01:24:41.290
Yeah, they,

01:24:41.290 --> 01:24:44.370
I think they understood that our hearts

01:24:44.370 --> 01:24:45.370
were in the right place,

01:24:45.370 --> 01:24:47.240
but they were already hurting so much.

01:24:47.240 --> 01:24:49.280
It was like, I do--

01:24:49.280 --> 01:24:51.180
I think they gave us
the benefit of the doubt

01:24:51.180 --> 01:24:52.769
because, I mean listen.

01:24:52.769 --> 01:24:53.887
Yeah.

01:24:53.887 --> 01:24:55.961
They lived in Buies Creek, North Carolina

01:24:55.961 --> 01:24:58.860
for a long time.

01:24:58.860 --> 01:25:02.860
And they knew that we
were sincere believers

01:25:02.860 --> 01:25:04.860
who cared about this guy,

01:25:04.860 --> 01:25:06.960
and saw the world in that way,

01:25:06.960 --> 01:25:09.900
and just wanted to see him come to faith.

01:25:09.900 --> 01:25:12.382
I think it probably was
a little bit annoying

01:25:12.382 --> 01:25:14.320
because I don't know,

01:25:14.320 --> 01:25:16.250
I don't know where they are,

01:25:16.250 --> 01:25:17.523
or where they were at the time.

01:25:17.523 --> 01:25:20.950
But I have, they always
seemed to be really thoughtful

01:25:23.760 --> 01:25:27.290
people who didn't just
acquiesce to the status quo,

01:25:27.290 --> 01:25:28.790
which the status quo in Buies Creek

01:25:28.790 --> 01:25:32.610
was you're gonna be a
Bible believing Christian.

01:25:32.610 --> 01:25:35.259
And so, I do, you know,

01:25:35.259 --> 01:25:37.080
I don't know.

01:25:37.080 --> 01:25:38.320
I feel like

01:25:40.750 --> 01:25:42.971
there's a part of me that
wants to apologize, you know.

01:25:42.971 --> 01:25:44.590
I think that,

01:25:44.590 --> 01:25:45.423
you know, in these--

01:25:45.423 --> 01:25:46.810
On behalf of my former self.

01:25:46.810 --> 01:25:49.010
In these, my thought on that is

01:25:49.010 --> 01:25:50.670
in these books, you know,

01:25:50.670 --> 01:25:52.020
as we've shared at the top,

01:25:53.640 --> 01:25:55.100
we've really,

01:25:55.100 --> 01:25:57.020
we really sought to honor Ben

01:25:57.020 --> 01:26:01.103
because of how important he is to us.

01:26:02.080 --> 01:26:03.870
But I do think that

01:26:03.870 --> 01:26:05.833
there's also a part of it that's like,

01:26:07.670 --> 01:26:10.660
it's the best we can do to

01:26:10.660 --> 01:26:13.670
make amends for the regrets that we have,

01:26:13.670 --> 01:26:16.750
and again, it's not just
the spiritual stuff,

01:26:16.750 --> 01:26:18.650
but it's just you know,

01:26:18.650 --> 01:26:20.280
not being capable of

01:26:22.045 --> 01:26:26.060
being the friend that he deserved.

01:26:26.060 --> 01:26:27.570
Yeah.

01:26:27.570 --> 01:26:32.480
And over many years since his diagnosis,

01:26:32.480 --> 01:26:34.003
his original diagnosis.

01:26:36.030 --> 01:26:36.880
And you know, so,

01:26:38.023 --> 01:26:40.990
I think that, I think it's
been our best effort to,

01:26:40.990 --> 01:26:42.363
to honor him and to,

01:26:44.107 --> 01:26:47.863
but yeah, I am sorry for,

01:26:51.460 --> 01:26:53.350
and I know he forgave us.

01:26:53.350 --> 01:26:54.960
We did discuss it

01:26:54.960 --> 01:26:56.670
at his bedside.

01:26:56.670 --> 01:26:58.170
We told him we were sorry--

01:26:58.170 --> 01:26:59.517
For not being there for him.

01:26:59.517 --> 01:27:00.350
For not being there for him.

01:27:00.350 --> 01:27:02.534
And he forgave us.

01:27:02.534 --> 01:27:03.367
Yeah.

01:27:06.680 --> 01:27:09.633
That's the kind of person that he is.

01:27:11.510 --> 01:27:14.163
And you know, so,

01:27:16.680 --> 01:27:19.653
I think that everything that we do,

01:27:21.084 --> 01:27:22.423
he touches.

01:27:23.340 --> 01:27:24.603
And I think that's the,

01:27:28.350 --> 01:27:31.187
that's the best we can do to honor him.

01:27:32.160 --> 01:27:33.300
It's what we are,

01:27:33.300 --> 01:27:34.470
what we have done.

01:27:34.470 --> 01:27:37.680
Yeah, I think the only
thing I'll add to that

01:27:39.020 --> 01:27:41.983
is that you know, we don't know,

01:27:43.470 --> 01:27:44.830
we don't know what's next

01:27:46.580 --> 01:27:48.533
after this life.

01:27:50.360 --> 01:27:54.000
But there's one thing I do
know with certainty is that

01:27:54.000 --> 01:27:55.213
Ben lives on.

01:28:00.170 --> 01:28:01.430
And his legacy

01:28:04.320 --> 01:28:05.650
is what we try to honor,

01:28:05.650 --> 01:28:06.950
and what we try to embody.

01:28:12.890 --> 01:28:15.560
And with that, I'll give a recommendation,

01:28:15.560 --> 01:28:17.200
which is along these same lines.

01:28:17.200 --> 01:28:18.033
Okay.

01:28:22.190 --> 01:28:23.263
We've all got,

01:28:29.060 --> 01:28:31.493
we've all got Bens in our lives.

01:28:34.500 --> 01:28:36.783
And it doesn't have to be somebody who,

01:28:38.290 --> 01:28:40.350
you know, is sick, or is dying.

01:28:40.350 --> 01:28:41.253
It might be.

01:28:43.660 --> 01:28:45.010
But just somebody who is

01:28:47.708 --> 01:28:49.403
been a friend and impacted you,

01:28:50.730 --> 01:28:51.563
and,

01:28:56.150 --> 01:28:58.270
maybe some circumstances of life

01:28:58.270 --> 01:29:00.793
have led you away from them,

01:29:01.779 --> 01:29:03.513
and you haven't connected
with them in a while.

01:29:04.960 --> 01:29:06.997
Make an effort to connect with them.

01:29:16.420 --> 01:29:17.493
Okay, well,

01:29:20.340 --> 01:29:21.443
thanks for listening.

01:29:24.582 --> 01:29:25.960
If you wanna contribute
to this conversation,

01:29:25.960 --> 01:29:26.793
you know what to do,

01:29:26.793 --> 01:29:27.626
#EarBiscuits.

01:29:31.957 --> 01:29:34.860
We'll, we'll talk at you next week.

01:29:34.860 --> 01:29:36.560
And probably not cry next week.

01:29:36.560 --> 01:29:37.483
I don't know.

